export const ARTICLES: HelpArticle[] = [
  {"id":"what-do-you-need-from-us-to-get-started","cat":"start","status":"m","asked":28,"q":"What do you need from us to get started?","a":"<p>Three separate actions, and the setup cannot begin until all three are done:</p><ol><li>Share your Shopify <strong>collaborator code</strong> so we can raise an access request.</li><li><strong>Approve</strong> the request when it lands in your admin. Roles are pre selected in the request, so you only need to open it and accept.</li><li><strong>Install the app</strong> from the private app link. Approving access does not install anything, and this is the step most stores miss.</li></ol><p>After that we read your store data, take a copy of your live theme, and configure plans on the copy.</p>","path":"Settings > Collaborators, then the private app link we send you","ord":0},
  {"id":"what-happens-after-we-give-access-and-install-the-app","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":22,"q":"What happens after we give access and install the app?","a":"<p>Three things run, and two of them overlap:</p><ul><li>You get a <strong>look and feel preview</strong> quickly, using placeholder plans, so you can react to the widget and landing pages before pricing is settled.</li><li>In parallel we read your order history and send a <strong>plan sheet</strong>: proposed durations, delivery frequencies and discount bands, with the repeat purchase gap we found in your data.</li><li>Then a <strong>final preview</strong> with your real plans in place, plus a call for last mile edits.</li></ul><p>Go live happens only after you say so.</p>","path":"","ord":1},
  {"id":"will-anything-go-live-on-our-store-without-our-approval","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":9,"q":"Will anything go live on our store without our approval?","a":"<p>No. All configuration happens on a duplicate of your theme, and your live theme is untouched until you give the word. If you would rather we work on a specific theme (a draft you are preparing, or a non sale backup copy), say so and we will build there instead.</p><p>One exception worth flagging: changes inside a page builder such as PageFly or GemPages do go live, because those builders publish directly. We will tell you before touching one, and we will tell you exactly what we are changing.</p>","path":"","ord":2},
  {"id":"why-has-my-preview-link-expired","cat":"start","status":"l","asked":11,"q":"Why has my preview link expired?","a":"<p>Preview links are Shopify theme preview links and they stop working after about 48 hours. Nothing is wrong with your setup. Ask in your store group and we will issue a fresh link.</p><p>If a link looks wrong rather than expired, open it in an incognito window first. A cached older version of the widget or a stale discount is the most common cause of a preview that disagrees with what we just told you we changed.</p>","path":"","ord":3},
  {"id":"can-we-hold-go-live-until-our-new-theme-site-or-checkout-is-read","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":10,"q":"Can we hold go-live until our new theme, site or checkout is ready?","a":"<p>Yes, and several pilot stores did exactly that. We stay ready and wait. Plans, widgets and landing pages can all be configured now and parked, then restyled to the new theme when it publishes.</p><p>If you are launching a new checkout partner at the same time, going live a day apart is sensible: launch the checkout, watch it for 24 hours, then bring subscriptions on.</p>","path":"","ord":4},
  {"id":"which-stores-are-already-live-on-stackback","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Which stores are already live on StackBack?","a":"<p>Live merchant references we share on request include <strong>milld.com</strong>, <strong>thestack.club</strong>, <strong>thebasicswoman.com</strong>, <strong>everpurelife.com</strong> and <strong>satturmittaikadai.com</strong>. Ask your activation contact for the ones closest to your category.</p><p>If you spot the widget on a store we have not mentioned, ask. At least one brand was running a manual subscription programme on spreadsheets that looked similar from the outside.</p>","path":"","ord":5},
  {"id":"can-we-sign-an-nda-before-sharing-store-access","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can we sign an NDA before sharing store access?","a":"<p>Yes. Send it over or ask us for ours and we will get it signed before you share the collaborator code. Several pilot stores did this first and it did not delay onboarding by more than a day.</p>","path":"","ord":6},
  {"id":"can-we-test-the-whole-flow-ourselves-before-going-live","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":7,"q":"Can we test the whole flow ourselves before going live?","a":"<p>Yes, and you should. Open the preview link, pick a plan, click Subscribe now and complete checkout. You will see the widget, the cart, the checkout, the confirmation and the customer portal exactly as a customer would.</p><p>Two caveats: <strong>real payments will not go through on a preview URL</strong> (see the payments section), and if you want to test the reminder flow we can give you a plan option that delivers every few minutes so you do not have to wait a month.</p>","path":"","ord":7},
  {"id":"can-we-take-a-real-test-payment-on-the-preview-link","cat":"start","status":"n","asked":4,"q":"Can we take a real test payment on the preview link?","a":"<p>No. Payment gateways reject transactions on a Shopify preview URL, so the checkout will fail there no matter how the app is configured. This is a gateway rule, not a StackBack limitation.</p><p>The workaround we use is a dummy product published unlisted on your live site. Its prices and buttons are deliberately meaningless, it exists only to prove payment capture, order creation and the mandate flow work end to end. Ignore its maths and read the real preview link for pricing.</p>","path":"","ord":8},
  {"id":"can-we-go-live-on-a-hidden-page-first-rather-than-the-real-produ","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we go live on a hidden page first rather than the real product page?","a":"<p>Yes. We publish an unlisted product page on your live site so your team can transact against it with real payments. Once you are satisfied, we add the widget to the real product pages.</p>","path":"","ord":9},
  {"id":"will-you-help-promote-the-subscription-launch","cat":"start","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"Will you help promote the subscription launch?","a":"<p>Run the campaigns with your own performance team, since they know your audience and creative. We help with the <strong>messaging</strong>: what to say about the plan, how to frame the saving, which page to send traffic to.</p><p>For subscription specific ads, send traffic to the subscription landing page rather than the product page. That page opens with the plan already selected, so you are not paying for clicks that land on a one time purchase view.</p>","path":"","ord":10},
  {"id":"how-fast-can-a-change-be-rolled-back-after-go-live","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"How fast can a change be rolled back after go live?","a":"<p>Configuration changes, such as reverting to a previous plan setup, switching the default back to one time purchase, or removing the widget, happen within minutes of your asking.</p><p>Changes that need a code deployment go out in the next production push, usually overnight, because a mid day deployment takes the app down briefly for every store on it.</p>","path":"","ord":11},
  {"id":"our-previous-subscription-app-has-live-subscribers-can-they-move","cat":"start","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Our previous subscription app has live subscribers. Can they move across?","a":"<p>Yes, this has been done. We take the subscription data out of the old app, import the contracts into StackBack in parallel with your setup, and then the old app is disabled at go live.</p><p>Expect a tail: recurring invoice emails the old app already scheduled can still land and still create orders for a few days after the switch. That is the old app finishing its queue, not StackBack double billing.</p>","path":"","ord":12},
  {"id":"what-happens-the-moment-a-customer-subscribes","cat":"flows","status":"w","asked":11,"q":"What happens the moment a customer subscribes","a":"<figure class=\"flow\"><div class=\"scroller\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 208\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One subscription checkout creates a parent order that collects payment and is auto fulfilled, a subscription contract holding the delivery timeline, and one child order per due delivery which is the order that actually ships.\"><defs><marker id=\"ar2\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 1 L9 5 L0 9 z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"10\" y=\"30\" width=\"150\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Customer</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"73\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">completes checkout</text><rect x=\"212\" y=\"30\" width=\"186\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--warn);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" letter-spacing=\"1\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">ORDER 1</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Parent order</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"73\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">payment collected</text><rect x=\"450\" y=\"30\" width=\"186\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"543\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" letter-spacing=\"1\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">CONTRACT</text><text x=\"543\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Delivery timeline</text><text x=\"543\" y=\"73\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">1 row per delivery</text><rect x=\"688\" y=\"30\" width=\"182\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--ok);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"779\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" letter-spacing=\"1\" style=\"fill:var(--ok)\">ORDER 2..N</text><text x=\"779\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Child order</text><text x=\"779\" y=\"73\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">one per delivery</text><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"61\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#ar2)\"/><line x1=\"403\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"443\" y2=\"61\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#ar2)\"/><line x1=\"641\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"681\" y2=\"61\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#ar2)\"/><text x=\"423\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">creates</text><text x=\"661\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">when due</text><line x1=\"305\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"305\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"112\" width=\"186\" height=\"82\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--warn-soft);stroke:var(--warn);stroke-width:1\"/><text x=\"224\" y=\"130\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">auto fulfilled</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"146\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">shipping not required</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"162\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">helper line, no stock</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"178\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">filter out of reports</text><line x1=\"779\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"779\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/><rect x=\"688\" y=\"112\" width=\"182\" height=\"82\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--ok-soft);stroke:var(--ok);stroke-width:1\"/><text x=\"700\" y=\"130\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--ok)\">real product</text><text x=\"700\" y=\"146\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--ok)\">left unfulfilled</text><text x=\"700\" y=\"162\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--ok)\">carries the shipping</text><text x=\"700\" y=\"178\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--ok)\">your 3PL ships this</text></svg></div><figcaption>This is by design, not a duplicate-order bug. Shopify needs a real order to collect the subscription payment and hold the contract, so that order carries a helper line item, is marked <b>shipping not required</b> and is auto fulfilled, which keeps it out of your 3PL queue. The order your warehouse should act on is the child order, and it is the one that carries shipping.</figcaption></figure><p>Read the child order, not the parent, when you are deciding what to ship. In your reporting, filter the parent orders out: they carry a helper line item that is not tracked for inventory, so they do not touch stock, but they do show as paid orders.</p>","path":"","ord":13},
  {"id":"how-pay-as-you-go-billing-decides-to-charge-or-pause","cat":"flows","status":"w","asked":9,"q":"How pay as you go billing decides to charge or pause","a":"<figure class=\"flow\"><div class=\"scroller\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 236\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pay as you go billing sends a series of reminders before each delivery date. If the customer pays, the delivery order is created and a paused subscription resumes. If the delivery date arrives unpaid, the whole subscription is paused with no grace period.\"><defs><marker id=\"ar3\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 1 L9 5 L0 9 z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></marker></defs><text x=\"40\" y=\"24\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9.5\" letter-spacing=\"1\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">REMINDERS, ONE EVERY reminder_gap</text><line x1=\"42\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"556\" y2=\"86\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"86\" r=\"4\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"78\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">invoice</text><circle cx=\"190\" cy=\"86\" r=\"4\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">reminder</text><circle cx=\"302\" cy=\"86\" r=\"4\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">reminder</text><circle cx=\"414\" cy=\"86\" r=\"4\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"414\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">reminder</text><line x1=\"546\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"546\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"var(--ink-3)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"546\" y=\"44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"9.5\" letter-spacing=\".5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">DELIVERY DATE</text><path d=\"M556 82 L596 46\" stroke=\"var(--ok)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ar3)\"/><path d=\"M556 92 L596 150\" stroke=\"var(--warn)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ar3)\"/><rect x=\"604\" y=\"12\" width=\"264\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ok-soft);stroke:var(--ok);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"618\" y=\"36\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--ok)\">Paid before the date</text><text x=\"618\" y=\"55\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">delivery order created, subscription</text><text x=\"618\" y=\"69\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">resumes if it was paused</text><rect x=\"604\" y=\"122\" width=\"264\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--warn-soft);stroke:var(--warn);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"618\" y=\"146\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">Still unpaid on the date</text><text x=\"618\" y=\"165\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">whole subscription pauses and its</text><text x=\"618\" y=\"179\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">queued jobs are cleared</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"152\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">first invoice = delivery date - time_to_delivery - payg_reminder</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-3)\">defaults: time_to_delivery 7 days, reminders repeat every reminder_gap</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"196\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--warn)\">There is no grace period after the delivery date.</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"213\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">The pause check runs on the date itself, so set your reminder window with that in mind.</text></svg></div><figcaption>Only two outcomes are reachable for an unpaid pay as you go delivery: <b>paid</b> or <b>subscription paused</b>. Reminder timing is per store, set under Settings, so if customers feel rushed the fix is a wider <code>payg_reminder</code> window, not a change to the pause rule.</figcaption></figure><p>Practical consequence for your support team: a customer who says \"I was paused without warning\" almost always did receive the invoice and the reminders, and the reminder window was simply too short for them. Widening <code>payg_reminder</code> fixes that class of complaint.</p>","path":"App > Settings > timing configuration","ord":14},
  {"id":"what-happens-when-a-delivery-is-edited","cat":"flows","status":"l","asked":5,"q":"What happens when a delivery is edited","a":"<figure class=\"flow\"><div class=\"scroller\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 232\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Editing a delivery compares the new basket against the old one. If it costs more the customer gets a payment link and the change applies on payment. If it costs the same the change applies immediately. If it costs less it becomes a refund, which only the merchant can action.\"><defs><marker id=\"ar4\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 1 L9 5 L0 9 z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"10\" y=\"84\" width=\"152\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"86\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Swap, add or</text><text x=\"86\" y=\"127\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">remove an item</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"84\" width=\"152\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--accent-soft);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"276\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\">Price difference</text><text x=\"276\" y=\"127\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\">for that delivery</text><line x1=\"167\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"193\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#ar4)\"/><rect x=\"400\" y=\"14\" width=\"468\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"414\" y=\"34\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Costs more</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"50\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">Customer gets a payment link. The change applies</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"64\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">the moment that payment lands, not before.</text><rect x=\"400\" y=\"88\" width=\"468\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"414\" y=\"108\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Costs the same</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"124\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">Applied immediately, no payment step and no</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"138\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">customer action needed.</text><rect x=\"400\" y=\"162\" width=\"468\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface-2);stroke:var(--line-2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"414\" y=\"182\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Costs less</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"198\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">Becomes a refund, store credit or direct. Only</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"212\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\">the merchant can action this, not the customer.</text><path d=\"M357 108 L392 45\"  stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ar4)\"/><path d=\"M357 115 L392 119\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ar4)\"/><path d=\"M357 122 L392 193\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ar4)\"/></svg></div><figcaption>The rule that surprises people: an edit that costs more is <b>not applied when it is requested</b>. It waits on the payment link. Until the customer pays, the delivery still holds the old basket, so check the delivery in Purchase Contracts before you pick and pack.</figcaption></figure><p>Customers can trigger edits that cost the same or more from the portal. Refunds are merchant only, because they move money out.</p>","path":"","ord":15},
  {"id":"the-states-a-subscription-can-be-in","cat":"flows","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"The states a subscription can be in","a":"<p>A subscription is in exactly one of four states:</p><ul><li><strong>Active</strong>: deliveries are scheduled and will be created as they come due.</li><li><strong>Paused</strong>: deliveries stop and every queued job for that subscription is cleared. A pay as you go subscription pauses itself on an unpaid delivery date. Paying the outstanding invoice resumes it automatically.</li><li><strong>Cancelled</strong>: terminal. Unused prepaid deliveries are refunded as part of the cancellation.</li><li><strong>Completed</strong>: every delivery in the plan shipped. Terminal, and the good outcome.</li></ul><p>Each delivery inside the timeline has its own status, which is what the timeline view in Purchase Contracts shows you: pending until the Shopify order exists, then fulfilled once Shopify reports it shipped.</p>","path":"App > Purchase Contracts > open a contract > timeline","ord":16},
  {"id":"where-do-i-find-the-collaborator-code","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":14,"q":"Where do I find the collaborator code?","a":"<p>In your Shopify admin, go to <strong>Settings</strong> and search for \"collaborators\". The code is shown on that page. Send us that code and we raise the request from our side.</p><p>If you would rather we walk you through it, ask for a five minute call. Several stores found that faster than messaging screenshots back and forth.</p>","path":"Shopify admin > Settings > Users > Collaborators","ord":17},
  {"id":"which-permissions-do-you-need-and-which-can-i-withhold","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":12,"q":"Which permissions do you need, and which can I withhold?","a":"<p>Full access during setup is easiest, and permissions can be tightened afterwards. If you would rather scope it now, these are the ones the setup genuinely needs:</p><ul><li><strong>Products</strong> and <strong>Orders</strong>: to read purchase patterns and configure plans. Order data is not optional, the plan proposals are built from it.</li><li><strong>Apps</strong> (manage and install apps and channels): without it we cannot see whether StackBack is installed, so we cannot confirm your step 3 is done.</li><li><strong>Themes</strong>: to duplicate your theme and place the widget.</li><li><strong>Markets</strong>: only if you sell internationally, so we can read your shipping profiles and rates.</li></ul><p>You can deselect <strong>app charges</strong>. Stores have also asked us to drop <strong>customer data</strong> and <strong>analytics</strong>, and we re-raised the request without them, so those two are negotiable if order access stays.</p>","path":"","ord":18},
  {"id":"i-approved-the-request-is-anything-still-pending","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":9,"q":"I approved the request. Is anything still pending?","a":"<p>Probably the install. Approving collaborator access and installing the app are two separate actions, and approving one does not do the other. Open the private app link we sent and install from there.</p><p>If both are genuinely done and we still say we cannot see the app, the missing piece is Apps access on the collaborator role, which is what lets us verify the install.</p>","path":"","ord":19},
  {"id":"the-collaborator-code-is-not-working","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":5,"q":"The collaborator code is not working","a":"<p>Most often the request is being raised against the wrong store URL. Brands frequently have more than one Shopify domain, and the code belongs to one specific store. Confirm the exact myshopify domain you want subscriptions on and we will re-raise against it.</p><p>Shopify admin itself also has occasional wobbles. If the code is right and the request still fails, wait an hour and try again before we escalate.</p>","path":"","ord":20},
  {"id":"you-say-no-permissions-were-granted-but-i-granted-them","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"You say no permissions were granted, but I granted them","a":"<p>This shows up when the role exists but its permission set did not save. It is usually not one missing checkbox, it reads as if nothing was granted at all.</p><p>The reliable fix is to remove StackBack as a collaborator entirely, then let us re-raise the request with the roles pre selected. Editing the existing role in place is what tends to fail silently.</p>","path":"Shopify admin > Settings > Users > find the role > Edit","ord":21},
  {"id":"can-we-skip-collaborator-access-and-just-add-your-email-as-staff","cat":"access","status":"n","asked":4,"q":"Can we skip collaborator access and just add your email as staff?","a":"<p>We work off collaborator access, so send the code and approve the request. Adding an email as a staff account does not give us the app and theme context the setup needs, and it consumes one of your staff seats.</p>","path":"","ord":22},
  {"id":"we-run-two-stores-do-we-do-all-of-this-twice","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"We run two stores. Do we do all of this twice?","a":"<p>Yes. Each Shopify store needs its own collaborator code, its own approval and its own app install, and each gets its own plans, widget and previews. Nothing carries across automatically.</p>","path":"","ord":23},
  {"id":"the-app-link-will-not-open-or-the-install-fails","cat":"access","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"The app link will not open, or the install fails","a":"<p>This has happened on a handful of stores and it is almost always a browser or permission state on your side rather than the link. The fastest resolution is a short call with whoever manages your Shopify admin, screen shared. Ask for it rather than retrying for a day.</p>","path":"","ord":24},
  {"id":"our-collaborator-access-was-revoked-what-stops-working","cat":"access","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"Our collaborator access was revoked. What stops working?","a":"<p>Everything we do on your behalf. Without access we cannot reschedule a delivery, pause a subscription for a customer, action a cancellation, fix a widget or check an order, so every request in the group queues up until access is restored.</p><p>Access has been revoked accidentally more than once during unrelated admin cleanups. If our updates suddenly go quiet, check the collaborator list first.</p>","path":"","ord":25},
  {"id":"can-we-restrict-your-permissions-once-we-are-live","cat":"access","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can we restrict your permissions once we are live?","a":"<p>Yes. Setup is the phase that needs the widest access. Once you are live and stable, tighten the role. Keep Orders and Apps if you want us to keep actioning customer requests and diagnosing issues for you.</p>","path":"","ord":26},
  {"id":"what-payment-options-can-we-offer-and-which-should-we-pick","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":24,"q":"What payment options can we offer, and which should we pick?","a":"<p>Two, and you can enable either or both:</p><ul><li><strong>Prepaid</strong>: the customer pays for the whole plan upfront. Revenue lands immediately, churn is effectively zero for the plan length, and there is no invoice to chase.</li><li><strong>Pay as you go</strong>: the customer pays for the first delivery, then gets a payment link before each subsequent one. Lower friction at signup, higher drop off later, because a customer who stops paying stops the plan.</li></ul><p>Our read across the pilot: first time customers pick pay as you go more often, so it works as an acquisition option, while prepaid is what actually produces predictable revenue. If you can only support one at launch, most stores chose prepaid.</p><p>A third mode, <strong>UPI AutoPay</strong> through Razorpay, removes the invoice chasing from pay as you go. See the payments section.</p>","path":"","ord":27},
  {"id":"do-we-have-to-choose-between-subscriptions-and-bundles","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Do we have to choose between subscriptions and bundles?","a":"<p>No. Run either or both. They answer different problems: subscriptions build repeat revenue on a product a customer already rebuys, bundles lift the value of a single order.</p><p>If your products have genuinely different delivery rhythms (a monthly staple and a fortnightly one), bundling them together creates a fulfilment headache, so subscriptions only is a reasonable call.</p>","path":"","ord":28},
  {"id":"what-delivery-frequency-and-plan-length-should-we-use","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":12,"q":"What delivery frequency and plan length should we use?","a":"<p>We propose these from your own order history rather than a template, by measuring the gap between repeat purchases. Real examples from the pilot: a 28 to 35 day gap produced monthly delivery on 3, 6 and 12 month plans; a 12 to 18 day gap produced a fortnightly plan; a consumable bought weekly produced weekly delivery.</p><p>The plan sheet we send you has a tab mapping each product to its plan and its measured repeat cycle, so you can argue with the evidence rather than the conclusion. If you know something the data does not, say so and we change it.</p>","path":"","ord":29},
  {"id":"what-discount-should-we-set","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":11,"q":"What discount should we set?","a":"<p>The pattern that worked across the pilot: set the shortest plan slightly above your normal store discount, then add three to four points for each longer plan. If you usually give 10 percent, a 15 percent three month plan and a 19 percent six month plan reads as a real incentive.</p><p>On the longest plans, freebies tend to work better than more discount, because you are protecting margin while still increasing perceived value. For pay as you go, keep the rate slightly below prepaid, since the customer is committing to less.</p><p>Allow at least two to three weeks per pricing experiment before you read a result.</p>","path":"","ord":30},
  {"id":"can-different-products-have-different-discounts-and-durations","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":8,"q":"Can different products have different discounts and durations?","a":"<p>Yes. Discounts and plan lengths are set per plan, and a plan covers the products you assign to it, so you can run 8 percent on a six month plan for one product and 20 percent on a yearly plan for another.</p><p>Send the list as product against duration against discount and we will configure it exactly. Several stores sent a spreadsheet and that is the fastest route.</p>","path":"","ord":31},
  {"id":"can-we-set-discounts-at-variant-level","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":5,"q":"Can we set discounts at variant level?","a":"<p>Not per individual variant, but you can split variants into groups with different rates. For example the 10ml, 250ml and 500ml variants at one rate and the rest at another. That covers most of what stores actually want.</p><p>If it is a single awkward variant, the cleaner answer is often to exclude it from subscriptions entirely.</p>","path":"","ord":32},
  {"id":"can-we-exclude-a-product-or-a-variant-from-subscriptions","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Can we exclude a product or a variant from subscriptions?","a":"<p>Yes, both. Excluded products simply show no subscription option on their page, which is the right answer for anything you do not want on a recurring plan, whether that is a one off treatment product or a variant with thin margin.</p><p>Several stores also chose to launch on best sellers only and add more products later. That is a good idea: it keeps the first month of fulfilment simple.</p>","path":"","ord":33},
  {"id":"can-we-set-a-fixed-subscription-price-instead-of-a-discount","cat":"plans","status":"n","asked":4,"q":"Can we set a fixed subscription price instead of a discount?","a":"<p>No. Prices live in Shopify and StackBack applies a discount rate on top of them, in the same way a discount code does. It cannot assign an absolute price to a variant.</p><p>If you want a subscription to land on a round number, back calculate the rate from that number and we will configure the rate. That is how stores have got to prices like 1449, 1349 and 1249 across three plan lengths.</p>","path":"","ord":34},
  {"id":"can-discounts-grow-with-each-delivery-cycle","cat":"plans","status":"r","asked":3,"q":"Can discounts grow with each delivery cycle?","a":"<p>Not today. A plan carries one rate for its whole length, so a ladder of 7 percent in month one rising to 15 percent by month six cannot be configured. It is on the roadmap.</p><p>The workaround stores adopted: a flat rate for the plan, with <strong>different freebies at different cycles</strong>. That produces the same rising sense of reward while keeping the discount predictable, and it is often better for margin.</p>","path":"","ord":35},
  {"id":"can-we-add-freebies-to-a-plan","cat":"plans","status":"m","asked":7,"q":"Can we add freebies to a plan?","a":"<p>Yes, and this is one of the most effective levers in the pilot. Freebies attach to a specific delivery, so you can give a shaker on delivery three and a full size product on delivery six.</p><p>Two things you have to do:</p><ul><li>Every freebie must exist as a <strong>product or a gift card in Shopify</strong>, because that is what gets pushed into the order. If it is not in your catalogue, send us an image and we will create it.</li><li>Keep freebie products <strong>unlisted</strong>, or customers will find and buy them. This has happened, and it was our error, but check yours after setup.</li></ul><p>A \"mystery gift worth 2000\" can be created as a single product attached to a delivery, leaving you free to decide what actually goes in the box.</p>","path":"","ord":36},
  {"id":"can-we-show-the-value-of-the-freebies-on-the-plan","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we show the value of the freebies on the plan?","a":"<p>Yes. A \"freebies worth\" label can be added to each plan, which is a stronger nudge than the discount percentage on its own. You do not need to list the individual items on the label: tapping the free gift element already shows the customer the full list.</p>","path":"","ord":37},
  {"id":"will-our-existing-store-discounts-stack-on-top-of-subscription-d","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":9,"q":"Will our existing store discounts stack on top of subscription discounts?","a":"<p>By default the automatic discounts already running in your cart can stack, and this has produced orders at a much bigger discount than intended (one store saw prepaid land at roughly 35 percent). We can add an exclusion so cart level automatic discounts do not apply on subscription lines. Ask for it before you go live.</p><p>Discount codes are the other half: the discount configured on a plan is technically a discount code, so an additional code from the customer will not be valid on top of it. If your store already gives a permanent cart discount, tell us the number and we will set the subscription rate around it rather than on top of it.</p>","path":"","ord":38},
  {"id":"an-order-came-through-at-a-bigger-discount-than-we-set-what-happ","cat":"plans","status":"m","asked":5,"q":"An order came through at a bigger discount than we set. What happened?","a":"<p>Almost always discount stacking: an automatic cart discount or a payment offer applied on top of the plan rate. Send us the order number and we will confirm from the order timeline, then add the exclusion.</p><p>One store also had custom JavaScript in the theme that inserted a discount code into the cart, which fought with the plan discount and made the cart total disagree with the widget. If your theme does anything clever with discounts, mention it early.</p>","path":"","ord":39},
  {"id":"why-does-the-widget-show-635-55-rather-than-635","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":6,"q":"Why does the widget show 635.55 rather than 635?","a":"<p>Because a percentage discount on a Shopify price produces decimals, and the screens where money changes hands have to show the real amount. We have removed decimals from the display in several places, and we will do it for you, but a transacting screen will still show the true figure.</p><p>If a round number matters to you commercially, back calculate the discount rate from the price you want. Also check whether the number includes shipping: several \"the price is wrong\" reports turned out to be the shipping line, correctly added.</p>","path":"","ord":40},
  {"id":"can-you-make-a-specific-variant-the-default-on-the-product-page","cat":"plans","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Can you make a specific variant the default on the product page?","a":"<p>No, that is a Shopify setting. The default variant is whichever one your product configuration puts first, so it changes on your side, not in StackBack. Stores have also solved it by linking their subscription traffic to a URL that pre selects the variant.</p>","path":"","ord":41},
  {"id":"why-do-subscription-prices-change-when-the-customer-switches-pac","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Why do subscription prices change when the customer switches pack size?","a":"<p>Because the price is attached to the pack size variant in Shopify, and the plan applies its rate to whatever that variant costs. Changing the pack changes the base price, so the plan price moves with it. That is the intended behaviour for a store that prices by pack.</p><p>The mirror complaint also comes up: two variants that should differ in price do not, because Shopify has the same price on both. Check the variant prices in Shopify first, the widget is reflecting them faithfully.</p>","path":"","ord":42},
  {"id":"which-delivery-intervals-are-available","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Which delivery intervals are available?","a":"<p>Days, weeks and months, so weekly, fortnightly, every three weeks, monthly, every six weeks, every two months and quarterly are all configurable, along with the number of deliveries in the plan. Ask for the set you want and we will add them.</p><p>Resist offering everything. The stores with the cleanest first month picked two or three intervals that matched real repeat behaviour. More options mostly produced hesitation on the product page.</p>","path":"","ord":43},
  {"id":"can-we-cap-the-maximum-discount-shown-anywhere-on-the-site","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can we cap the maximum discount shown anywhere on the site?","a":"<p>Yes. Tell us the ceiling, for example 20 percent, and we will check every plan, widget label and landing page for anything advertising more than that. Copy left over from a placeholder configuration is the usual culprit.</p>","path":"","ord":44},
  {"id":"can-we-run-subscriptions-on-only-a-few-skus-to-start","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Can we run subscriptions on only a few SKUs to start?","a":"<p>Yes, and it is what we recommend. Send the SKU list and the rate each one should carry. Stores commonly launched on six SKUs, or on best sellers only, and expanded once the first cycle of deliveries had gone out cleanly.</p>","path":"","ord":45},
  {"id":"can-we-run-one-plan-across-a-group-of-products","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can we run one plan across a group of products?","a":"<p>Yes, and it is often necessary. One plan can cover a group of products, which is how a shared discount structure gets applied across a catalogue.</p><p>The trade off shows in the cart: because the plan is shared, the cart line carries the plan name rather than the specific product name. It stays specific enough to identify the plan (for example the serving size), but it will not read as the individual product title.</p>","path":"","ord":46},
  {"id":"what-is-the-difference-between-a-fixed-bundle-and-build-your-own","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":9,"q":"What is the difference between a fixed bundle and build your own?","a":"<p>A <strong>fixed bundle</strong> is a combination you decide: these three products, at this price, for this concern or occasion. A <strong>build your own</strong> bundle gives the customer a pool of products and a rule, for example pick any three from this category, with the discount rising as they add more.</p><p>You can run both at once, and both appear on the product page, presented through a dropdown on the bundle bar so they do not crowd each other.</p>","path":"","ord":47},
  {"id":"where-do-customers-find-the-bundles","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":8,"q":"Where do customers find the bundles?","a":"<p>Three routes, and most stores use at least two:</p><ul><li>A <strong>bundle bar on the product page</strong>, showing every bundle that contains that product.</li><li>A <strong>menu item</strong> in your storefront navigation, which we add for you.</li><li>The <strong>customer portal</strong>, which lists all bundles and subscriptions.</li></ul><p>Bundle landing pages sit under your own domain at a path beginning <code>/apps/stackback-portal</code>, so customers never leave your site.</p>","path":"yourdomain.com/apps/stackback-portal/lp/bundle-builder/","ord":48},
  {"id":"can-a-bundle-span-multiple-categories","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Can a bundle span multiple categories?","a":"<p>Yes, a bundle can draw from your whole catalogue or from several category pools at once. A cross category build your own, where the customer picks one item from each of three groups, needs configuring for your specific case, so it takes longer than a single category bundle.</p><p>The practical approach several stores took: launch the per category bundles immediately, and let the cross category one be built in parallel.</p>","path":"","ord":49},
  {"id":"can-fixed-bundles-and-build-your-own-run-side-by-side","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can fixed bundles and build your own run side by side?","a":"<p>Yes. Send both lists clearly separated and we will set the named combinations as fixed and the category pools as build your own. Be explicit, because a two product combination looks like a fixed bundle to us unless you say otherwise.</p>","path":"","ord":50},
  {"id":"how-does-the-tiered-bundle-discount-work","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":7,"q":"How does the tiered bundle discount work?","a":"<p>The discount rises with the number of items in the bundle, for example 5 percent at three products and 7 percent at five. The progress bar on the page shows the customer how close they are to the next tier, which is the point of it.</p><p>The tiers are a suggestion, not a requirement. If you would rather run one flat bundle discount, or discount only bundle subscriptions and not one time bundles, both are configurable.</p>","path":"","ord":51},
  {"id":"does-adding-more-quantity-of-one-product-move-the-discount-tier","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Does adding more quantity of one product move the discount tier?","a":"<p>Yes. Increasing the quantity of a single item counts toward tier progression, it does not require different products. Worth knowing before you set the tier thresholds, because a customer can reach the top tier with one SKU.</p>","path":"","ord":52},
  {"id":"can-the-bundle-discount-be-based-on-cart-value-instead-of-item-c","cat":"bundles","status":"r","asked":3,"q":"Can the bundle discount be based on cart value instead of item count?","a":"<p>Not yet. Tiers work on the number of products added, so \"10 percent above 3000 rupees\" cannot be configured as a bundle tier. It is on the roadmap.</p><p>Two things that do work in the meantime: a flat bundle discount regardless of count, and a free shipping threshold based on cart value, which covers a good part of the same intent.</p>","path":"","ord":53},
  {"id":"can-we-set-the-minimum-and-maximum-items-in-a-bundle","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can we set the minimum and maximum items in a bundle?","a":"<p>Yes, both. A build your own box can require at least one item and allow up to fifteen, or any range you specify.</p><p>What is not yet configurable is fixed step sizes, for example forcing a total of 15 units sold only in boxes of 2, 3 and 5. That is on the roadmap. Until then a minimum and maximum is the closest fit.</p>","path":"","ord":54},
  {"id":"bundles-we-did-not-ask-for-are-showing-as-fixed-bundles","cat":"bundles","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"Bundles we did not ask for are showing as fixed bundles","a":"<p>Any combination with only two products gets classified as a fixed bundle by default. If a two product pairing should be part of a build your own pool instead, tell us and we will move it.</p><p>The fastest way to avoid this is to send your bundle list as two explicit groups: the named combinations you want as fixed, and the category pools you want as build your own.</p>","path":"","ord":55},
  {"id":"can-we-rename-bundles","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we rename bundles?","a":"<p>Yes, any bundle title can be changed, including promotional wording such as \"Build your own, get 5 percent off plus extra 25 percent off at checkout\". Send the exact copy you want.</p><p>What cannot be done is putting that text onto the product image itself. Image overlays are not supported.</p>","path":"","ord":56},
  {"id":"can-pack-size-be-shown-in-the-bundle-picker","cat":"bundles","status":"r","asked":3,"q":"Can pack size be shown in the bundle picker?","a":"<p>Not yet. Where the same product is listed in several pack sizes, the picker shows the price but not the pack, which can genuinely confuse customers. It is on the roadmap.</p><p>Interim options: disable the side drawer so customers use the bundle landing page instead, where the listing is clearer, or rename the variants in Shopify so the pack size is part of the name we display.</p>","path":"","ord":57},
  {"id":"a-variant-dropdown-is-showing-in-our-build-your-own-box-and-we-d","cat":"bundles","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"A variant dropdown is showing in our build your own box and we do not want it","a":"<p>That dropdown comes from the variants configured on the product in Shopify, and variants can currently only be pulled from the product page. So the content of the dropdown is set on your side.</p><p>What we can change: remove the tiered discount percentages in favour of a fixed bundle discount. If you want variants shown individually alongside the product count instead of grouped behind a dropdown, the variant names have to be updated in Shopify first.</p>","path":"","ord":58},
  {"id":"can-one-bundle-show-variants-separately-while-another-groups-the","cat":"bundles","status":"r","asked":2,"q":"Can one bundle show variants separately while another groups them?","a":"<p>Not per bundle yet. The choice applies to all bundles: either grouped behind a dropdown everywhere, or listed individually everywhere. Per bundle configuration is on the roadmap.</p>","path":"","ord":59},
  {"id":"can-we-tell-customers-there-are-more-bundles-behind-the-bar","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can we tell customers there are more bundles behind the bar?","a":"<p>Yes. The bundle bar now shows a dropdown indicator plus the number of bundles available for that product, which was added after a store pointed out that customers assumed the first bundle was the only one.</p>","path":"","ord":60},
  {"id":"can-we-put-subscriptions-on-our-existing-combo-products","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can we put subscriptions on our existing combo products?","a":"<p>Yes. Existing combos and multi packs work as bundle subscriptions, so you do not need a separate bundle page if your combos already do that job. Several stores preferred this: subscriptions were added onto the combos they already sold, and the standalone bundle page was dropped.</p>","path":"","ord":61},
  {"id":"can-we-launch-the-subscription-widget-first-and-hold-bundles","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Can we launch the subscription widget first and hold bundles?","a":"<p>Yes, and the reverse too. Bundles can be configured and hidden until you are ready, and stores have gone live with the widget alone while bundle pages sat inactive. Say which you want visible and we will hide the rest.</p>","path":"","ord":62},
  {"id":"products-in-one-of-our-bundles-are-sold-out-what-now","cat":"bundles","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"Products in one of our bundles are sold out. What now?","a":"<p>Tell us which products to pull and we will take them out of the bundle. Sold out items left in a pool make the bundle feel broken to customers, and the bundle does not remove them automatically.</p>","path":"","ord":63},
  {"id":"can-we-have-several-bundle-pages-by-use-case","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we have several bundle pages by use case?","a":"<p>Yes. Multiple bundle pages can be created per use case or category and listed together under a Bundles menu item, and the generic shop tab can be removed from the portal so customers land on your curated pages instead. A product page banner leading to the bundle builder is optional on top of that.</p>","path":"","ord":64},
  {"id":"where-can-the-widget-sit-on-the-product-page","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":14,"q":"Where can the widget sit on the product page?","a":"<p>Two placements, both without the product name and quantity selector so it does not duplicate what your page already shows:</p><ul><li><strong>Above the purchase buttons</strong>, with one time purchase selected by default and the subscription options collapsed.</li><li><strong>Below the purchase buttons</strong>, already expanded.</li></ul><p>Which one wins depends on your page. If you have a sticky Buy now button, putting the widget above it tends to fight for attention, and the subscription landing page is often the better answer.</p>","path":"","ord":65},
  {"id":"can-we-build-the-subscription-options-into-our-own-custom-produc","cat":"widget","status":"n","asked":4,"q":"Can we build the subscription options into our own custom product page design?","a":"<p>No, and this is worth understanding rather than retrying. Reading the plan and discount data through an API is technically possible, so a custom design can display the right prices. What cannot be reproduced is the <strong>contract creation journey</strong>: the cart level events that turn a click into a subscription contract run as an app level custom function, which your developers cannot call.</p><p>So a custom widget would look right and then fail to create a subscription. We would rather adapt our widget to your design than let that happen.</p>","path":"","ord":66},
  {"id":"can-we-choose-a-different-widget-template","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":7,"q":"Can we choose a different widget template?","a":"<p>Only one template, Purchase Options, is live today, so there is currently no design choice. More templates are in progress, including one close to the compact reference several stores have sent us, and once they ship you will be able to switch templates without a rebuild.</p><p>In the meantime we adapt the live template to your brand: colours, corner radius, font size, label copy and which elements are shown.</p>","path":"","ord":67},
  {"id":"our-theme-does-not-support-blocks-can-we-still-have-the-widget","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Our theme does not support blocks. Can we still have the widget?","a":"<p>Not on the product page itself. Older or unofficial themes without block support cannot host the widget. The alternative we use is a <strong>banner on the product page</strong> that takes the customer to the subscription landing page, plus menu items for Subscribe and Bundles.</p><p>That configuration went live and worked. It is a real fallback, not a downgrade you have to accept quietly.</p>","path":"","ord":68},
  {"id":"our-theme-cannot-be-duplicated-without-breaking-the-site-what-th","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Our theme cannot be duplicated without breaking the site. What then?","a":"<p>We work on a copy of your theme by default, so a theme that crashes when duplicated needs a conversation with your developer before we touch anything. Themes built with hardcoded section and element IDs are the usual cause: duplication resets the IDs and the site breaks.</p><p>In that situation we work on the live theme with your team present, publish during low traffic, and validate through a walkthrough rather than a preview link, because external previews are not possible. Cleaning up the static section IDs is the real fix and worth doing before your next revamp.</p>","path":"","ord":69},
  {"id":"we-published-a-new-theme-does-the-widget-need-setting-up-again","cat":"widget","status":"m","asked":8,"q":"We published a new theme. Does the widget need setting up again?","a":"<p>Yes. The widget is placed in a theme, so a new or re-published theme needs the setup repeated. Tell us when the theme goes live and we redo the placement, usually the same day.</p><p>If you are mid revamp, we can build on your draft theme and move it to the live one when you publish. Several stores did that. Just tell us which theme to work on, since we will not guess between your live theme and a draft.</p>","path":"","ord":70},
  {"id":"can-the-widget-match-our-fonts-and-colours","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":9,"q":"Can the widget match our fonts and colours?","a":"<p>Yes. The widget uses your website font already, and we set the colours, corner radius and font sizes to match your theme. If your site is being redesigned, we restyle the widget against the new theme when it publishes rather than making you redo it.</p><p>Send a brand reference or just point us at the live site and we will match it.</p>","path":"","ord":71},
  {"id":"can-one-time-purchase-be-the-default-selection","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":9,"q":"Can one time purchase be the default selection?","a":"<p>Yes. We default to Subscribe and Save because a one time default means a lot of customers never notice the subscription, but this is a real trade off and it is your call.</p><p>Two stores saw conversion dip after subscribe became prominent and asked us to switch back to one time as the default. We did it within minutes. If your conversion rate is sensitive, start with one time as default and test the other way round once you have a baseline.</p>","path":"","ord":72},
  {"id":"can-add-to-cart-and-buy-now-hide-when-the-customer-selects-subsc","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can Add to Cart and Buy Now hide when the customer selects Subscribe and Save?","a":"<p>Yes, and it is now the behaviour. Leaving all three buttons visible was genuinely confusing: customers selected a plan and then clicked the theme Buy now button, which places a one time order at the one time price.</p>","path":"","ord":73},
  {"id":"why-are-there-two-buttons-subscribe-now-and-buy-now","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":6,"q":"Why are there two buttons, Subscribe now and Buy now?","a":"<p><strong>Subscribe now</strong> belongs to the widget and is what creates the subscription. <strong>Buy now</strong> belongs to your theme and always places a one time order, which is why it shows a different amount. The widget cannot control your theme button.</p><p>Three fixes, in order of preference: hide the theme buttons while subscribe is selected, add padding and a divider so the two are visually separate, or move subscriptions to the landing page if your theme has a sticky Buy now that cannot be separated.</p>","path":"","ord":74},
  {"id":"can-the-subscribe-now-button-be-removed","cat":"widget","status":"n","asked":2,"q":"Can the Subscribe now button be removed?","a":"<p>No. It is the button that places the subscription order, so removing it removes the ability to subscribe. The quantity selector inside the widget can be removed if your page already has one, and that is usually what stores actually wanted.</p>","path":"","ord":75},
  {"id":"can-we-change-the-widget-copy","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":12,"q":"Can we change the widget copy?","a":"<p>Much of it, yes. Delivered so far on request: \"Delivery every month\" under a plan, \"One time purchase\" instead of \"One time\", \"Extra 5 percent off\" instead of \"5 percent off\", the actual saving in rupees instead of a percentage, removing decimals, capitalising the labels and highlighting the discount callout in a stronger colour.</p><p>Some strings are shared across every live store, so changing them needs alignment across all of them and a testing cycle. When we say a copy change takes weeks rather than hours, that is why. Ask anyway, and we will tell you which category it falls into.</p>","path":"","ord":76},
  {"id":"can-we-remove-the-stackback-callout-from-the-widget","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can we remove the StackBack callout from the widget?","a":"<p>We remove the <strong>redirect</strong> on it on request, so no click takes the customer off your page. Several stores asked for exactly this and got it, including moving it to the bottom of the widget.</p><p>The callout itself stays. It is our main route to being discovered, and in the pilot it has not shown a measurable effect on conversion.</p>","path":"","ord":77},
  {"id":"why-is-the-widget-slow-to-load","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Why is the widget slow to load?","a":"<p>Plan and price data is fetched live rather than baked into the page, and the widget renders after the product data, so on a slow desktop connection you can watch it appear. On mobile, where most Indian D2C traffic sits, it lands inside the normal page load and is not noticeable in a real browsing flow.</p>","path":"","ord":78},
  {"id":"can-we-hide-the-widget-or-one-tab-in-it-after-launch","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Can we hide the widget, or one tab in it, after launch?","a":"<p>Yes, at any time. The widget is a block on the product page and can be hidden completely, and individual parts can be hidden too: the Bundle and Save tab, the Subscribe and Save tab, or a specific plan.</p><p>This is the right way to test. Configure the full set, launch, then hide whatever the data says is not earning its place.</p>","path":"","ord":79},
  {"id":"the-widget-is-not-showing-on-a-product-or-a-variant","cat":"widget","status":"m","asked":6,"q":"The widget is not showing on a product or a variant","a":"<p>Almost always no plan is assigned to that specific variant. A widget only appears where a plan exists, so a 6 pack variant with no plan shows nothing while the 500g and 1kg variants show the widget.</p><p>Send the product and variant and we will confirm. If a plan is assigned and it still does not show, check in incognito first, since a cached page is the second most common cause.</p>","path":"","ord":80},
  {"id":"what-image-sizes-do-you-need-for-landing-page-banners","cat":"widget","status":"m","asked":2,"q":"What image sizes do you need for landing page banners?","a":"<p>Desktop 1440 by 450 pixels, mobile 390 by 450 pixels. Send both, since the mobile crop of a desktop banner usually loses the message.</p>","path":"","ord":81},
  {"id":"can-the-subscription-section-go-anywhere-on-our-page-builder-pro","cat":"widget","status":"l","asked":5,"q":"Can the subscription section go anywhere on our page builder product page?","a":"<p>Not anywhere. On a page builder template the section can be placed at specific insertion points, which in practice has meant above the reviews block rather than immediately below a chosen content section.</p><p>The pattern that worked: put the section where it can go, then add a <strong>Subscribe now band</strong> higher up the page which scrolls the customer down to it. That gets you prominence without fighting the builder.</p>","path":"","ord":82},
  {"id":"will-you-change-things-in-our-page-builder-without-telling-us","cat":"widget","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"Will you change things in our page builder without telling us?","a":"<p>No, and we will name the changes first. Page builder edits are the one part of setup that publishes directly to your live site, so you should know what they are before we make them.</p><p>The two typical changes are adding a banner above your offers accordion, and moving a reviews block so the subscription section can sit above it. Test the section on a preview before we touch the builder. If something on your live page moves unexpectedly, tell us and we will restore it.</p>","path":"","ord":83},
  {"id":"can-we-add-our-own-subscription-faq-to-the-store","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can we add our own subscription FAQ to the store?","a":"<p>Yes. Send the FAQ document and we will add it, kept hidden in the template until you confirm go live. Product descriptions, FAQs and promotional cards can also be added to portal and landing pages.</p>","path":"","ord":84},
  {"id":"what-is-the-subscription-landing-page-for","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":7,"q":"What is the subscription landing page for?","a":"<p>A page that opens with the subscription plans already in front of the customer, without the one time purchase competing for the click. It is generated for you, sits on your domain and can be added to your menu.</p><p>Its best use is paid traffic. If you are running subscription specific ads, send them here rather than to the product page: the message matches the destination, and you avoid the conversion confusion of a page trying to do both jobs.</p>","path":"yourdomain.com/apps/stackback-portal/lp/{product-handle}","ord":85},
  {"id":"can-we-have-a-landing-page-without-adding-it-to-the-menu","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can we have a landing page without adding it to the menu?","a":"<p>Yes. The page can exist purely as a campaign destination, with no menu entry, if you do not want it in your navigation.</p>","path":"","ord":86},
  {"id":"can-a-product-page-banner-open-the-plans-for-that-specific-produ","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can a product page banner open the plans for that specific product?","a":"<p>Yes. Landing pages are addressed by product handle, so a banner can open the page for the product the customer is looking at rather than a general subscription dashboard. On page builders that support liquid blocks, the handle is passed through dynamically.</p>","path":"","ord":87},
  {"id":"does-cod-need-to-be-disabled-for-subscriptions","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":8,"q":"Does COD need to be disabled for subscriptions?","a":"<p>Yes, before you go live. If cash on delivery stays available on a subscription, payment can be collected at the doorstep on an order the customer has already paid for, or on a plan that was never prepaid. This is the single most consequential pre launch item on the merchant side.</p>","path":"","ord":88},
  {"id":"how-do-we-disable-cod-only-on-subscriptions-not-the-whole-store","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":6,"q":"How do we disable COD only on subscriptions, not the whole store?","a":"<p>Depends on your checkout:</p><ul><li><strong>Native Shopify COD</strong>: a free COD control app works. Stores have used Releasit COD Fee successfully, and we will help configure it once installed. You can uninstall it later if you stop needing it.</li><li><strong>Third party checkout</strong> such as GoKwik or Shopflo: use the checkout partner’s own product and collection exclusions, since a Shopify COD app often cannot see their COD option. Exclude the subscription products and collections there.</li></ul><p>We will send you the exact product and variant IDs to exclude. If nothing works on your stack, prepaid only is a legitimate launch configuration.</p>","path":"","ord":89},
  {"id":"does-stackback-work-with-our-third-party-checkout","cat":"pay","status":"l","asked":15,"q":"Does StackBack work with our third party checkout?","a":"<p>Yes for the product page widget. We have gone live alongside GoKwik, Shopflo, Shiprocket checkout and Razorpay, and the widget checkout can be routed through them.</p><p>The exception is the <strong>landing pages and the bundle pages</strong>, which are hosted on our portal and therefore use native Shopify checkout. If routing everything through your checkout partner is non negotiable, use the product page widget and we will disable the landing pages.</p><p>Setting this up takes coordination with your checkout partner, so raise it early rather than the week you want to launch.</p>","path":"","ord":90},
  {"id":"does-payment-routing-change-do-we-still-receive-money-the-same-w","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Does payment routing change? Do we still receive money the same way?","a":"<p>Nothing changes on your side. Payments are collected through your existing gateway into your existing account, and your operations, settlement and reconciliation stay exactly as they are. StackBack schedules and creates orders, it does not sit in your money flow.</p>","path":"","ord":91},
  {"id":"how-does-upi-autopay-work-and-what-do-you-need-from-us","cat":"pay","status":"l","asked":12,"q":"How does UPI AutoPay work, and what do you need from us?","a":"<p>AutoPay turns pay as you go into an automatic debit, so you stop chasing invoices. The customer approves a mandate once at signup and subsequent deliveries are debited.</p><p>What we need: your <strong>Razorpay credentials</strong>. Razorpay is the only gateway that can run recurring mandates with Shopify, so the store has to be onboarded there for AutoPay specifically, even if your normal checkout is someone else. We can make the introduction.</p><p>Status as of this build: AutoPay is live on pilot stores and being rolled out store by store with close monitoring, rather than switched on for everyone at once. Charging of the second and later deliveries is still being completed, so treat AutoPay as an active rollout and ask your contact where your store sits in it.</p>","path":"","ord":92},
  {"id":"why-do-you-need-razorpay-if-we-use-gokwik-or-shopflo","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Why do you need Razorpay if we use GoKwik or Shopflo?","a":"<p>Only for AutoPay. Your normal checkout stays as it is for one time and prepaid orders. Razorpay handles the recurring mandate because no other gateway currently supports mandates with Shopify subscriptions.</p>","path":"","ord":93},
  {"id":"can-autopay-run-through-gokwik","cat":"pay","status":"n","asked":2,"q":"Can AutoPay run through GoKwik?","a":"<p>No. GoKwik does not support recurring mandate purchases. There is a hybrid that technically works, a pay as you go subscription bought through GoKwik followed by a WhatsApp and email invitation to set up a Razorpay mandate, but we do not recommend it: the mandate only starts from the second payment and the customer can simply opt out, so you get the complexity without the reliability.</p>","path":"","ord":94},
  {"id":"will-easebuzz-or-payu-work-for-autopay","cat":"pay","status":"l","asked":1,"q":"Will Easebuzz or PayU work for AutoPay?","a":"<p>Not currently supported. Razorpay is the only mandate path we have built and tested. If your gateway relationship makes Razorpay difficult, raise it with your account contact rather than assuming a workaround exists.</p>","path":"","ord":95},
  {"id":"why-does-the-mandate-screen-show-a-bigger-amount-and-a-date-in-2","cat":"pay","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Why does the mandate screen show a bigger amount and a date in 2036?","a":"<p>Those are Razorpay’s own screens and they follow Razorpay’s compliance rules, so they cannot be edited, restyled or hidden. Every e-mandate carries a ceiling slightly above the actual charge and a long end date, so that a later price change does not require revoking the mandate and re-authenticating the customer.</p><p>It does look like a bigger commitment than it is, and that is a real conversion concern. What we can do is bring the ceiling down to the order amount, which we have done for stores that asked.</p>","path":"","ord":96},
  {"id":"can-the-autopay-limit-be-set-closer-to-the-order-value","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can the AutoPay limit be set closer to the order value?","a":"<p>Yes. The ceiling can be set to the order price rather than a large default. Make sure it still covers your highest priced SKU on that plan, otherwise a future upgrade will fail against the mandate.</p>","path":"","ord":97},
  {"id":"a-customer-set-up-autopay-but-the-backend-shows-pay-as-you-go","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"A customer set up AutoPay but the backend shows pay as you go","a":"<p>Not a bug, just timing. Razorpay takes a moment to confirm the mandate and the sync takes roughly a minute. Refresh and it shows as AutoPay. If it still reads as pay as you go after a few minutes, send us the subscription and we will check.</p>","path":"","ord":98},
  {"id":"checkout-is-not-picking-up-the-customer-s-saved-address","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"Checkout is not picking up the customer’s saved address","a":"<p>It should populate once the phone number is entered. If it does not, that is your checkout partner’s address lookup rather than the subscription, so raise it with them, and tell us too so we can rule out the cart payload.</p>","path":"","ord":99},
  {"id":"the-cart-total-does-not-match-the-total-shown-in-the-widget","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"The cart total does not match the total shown in the widget","a":"<p>Check three things in this order:</p><ol><li>Open it in incognito with a hard refresh. A cached discount is the most common cause.</li><li>Check whether shipping explains the gap. Several reports resolved to a correctly added shipping line.</li><li>Ask whether your theme has custom JavaScript that inserts a discount code into the cart. On one store that code fought with the plan discount and produced exactly this symptom.</li></ol><p>If none of those explain it, send the product, the plan and a screenshot of both screens.</p>","path":"","ord":100},
  {"id":"clicking-subscribe-now-says-the-item-was-added-but-the-cart-look","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Clicking Subscribe now says the item was added, but the cart looks empty","a":"<p>This was a cart refresh defect and it has been fixed. If you see it on a preview, hard refresh once and check again, then tell us if it persists, ideally with a screen recording.</p>","path":"","ord":101},
  {"id":"can-the-normal-add-to-cart-be-blocked-while-the-subscription-wid","cat":"pay","status":"r","asked":2,"q":"Can the normal Add to Cart be blocked while the subscription widget is open?","a":"<p>Hiding the theme Add to Cart and Buy Now when Subscribe and Save is selected is now supported, which solves most of this. Blocking the theme button in every other state is not configurable and sits on the roadmap.</p>","path":"","ord":102},
  {"id":"what-happens-if-a-pay-as-you-go-customer-never-pays","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"What happens if a pay as you go customer never pays?","a":"<p>The subscription pauses itself on the delivery date and every queued job for it is cleared, so nothing ships unpaid. Paying the outstanding invoice resumes it automatically. See the pay as you go flow in How it works for the full sequence.</p>","path":"","ord":103},
  {"id":"can-a-customer-effectively-cancel-by-just-not-paying","cat":"pay","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Can a customer effectively cancel by just not paying?","a":"<p>Yes, on pay as you go. Not paying the next invoice pauses the plan, so a customer can walk away after the first delivery having taken the subscription discount on it.</p><p>Two ways stores managed this. Keep pay as you go discounts lower than prepaid, and put the reward later in the plan, a freebie on the third or sixth delivery, so the customer only gets the good part by continuing. Or, as several stores decided, run prepaid only.</p>","path":"","ord":104},
  {"id":"what-are-the-new-subscription-plan-products-in-our-shopify-catal","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"What are the new subscription plan products in our Shopify catalogue?","a":"<p>Enabling Custom Checkout Adapt, which is what lets subscriptions run through a third party checkout, creates helper products named after the plan, for example \"3 Month Subscription Plan\". They exist to carry the plan price through the checkout.</p><p>Two things to do with them: <strong>exclude them from COD</strong>, and leave them alone otherwise. They are not stock tracked and do not require shipping, so they do not affect inventory. They do not need adding to your WMS or OMS.</p>","path":"App > Custom Checkout Adapt","ord":105},
  {"id":"why-did-one-subscription-create-two-orders","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Why did one subscription create two orders?","a":"<p>By design, and it is the single most common question we get. The first order is the <strong>parent order</strong>: Shopify requires a real order to collect the subscription payment and hold the contract, so it carries a helper line item, is marked shipping not required, and is auto fulfilled so your 3PL never picks it up. The second is the <strong>child order</strong>, the actual first delivery, left unfulfilled and carrying the shipping, and that is the one your warehouse should act on.</p><p>Both showing as paid is expected. Only one of them ships. The full picture is in <strong>How it works</strong>.</p>","path":"","ord":106},
  {"id":"will-the-parent-order-inflate-our-revenue-and-inventory-numbers","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Will the parent order inflate our revenue and inventory numbers?","a":"<p>Inventory, no. The parent order’s line item is not stock tracked, so it touches nothing in your catalogue. Revenue reporting, yes if you do not filter it, because it appears as a paid order.</p><p>Filter parent orders out of your revenue reports. They are identifiable by the helper line item. Ask us and we will show you the exact filter for your store.</p>","path":"","ord":107},
  {"id":"a-one-time-item-in-the-same-cart-as-a-subscription-was-auto-fulf","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"A one time item in the same cart as a subscription was auto fulfilled","a":"<p>This was a real gap and it is fixed. Partial fulfilment logic now keeps one time items in a mixed cart out of the auto fulfilment, so they stay unfulfilled and reach your 3PL.</p><p>If you see it on an older order, mark that line item unfulfilled manually and it will ship. Send us the order number so we can confirm it is not something new.</p>","path":"","ord":108},
  {"id":"when-does-the-next-delivery-order-appear-in-shopify","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"When does the next delivery order appear in Shopify?","a":"<p>Ahead of the delivery date, not on it, so your warehouse has time to pick and pack. The lead time is a per store setting, <code>time_to_delivery</code>, and defaults to 7 days.</p><p>So a delivery due on the 20th shows up as an order around the 13th. Several stores run it at 5 days instead, and 10 days works too. Tell us the number your warehouse needs and we will change the setting.</p><p>Only a <strong>paid</strong> delivery can be pushed. An unpaid pay as you go delivery cannot be turned into an order, by us or by you, which is what stops anything shipping unpaid.</p>","path":"App > Settings > timing configuration","ord":109},
  {"id":"can-we-push-a-delivery-order-to-shopify-early","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we push a delivery order to Shopify early?","a":"<p>Yes, and you can do it yourself. Open the app, go to <strong>Purchase Contracts</strong>, open the contract, select the delivery and push it to Shopify. Useful when a customer wants an early dispatch or you are clearing a batch before a holiday.</p>","path":"App > Purchase Contracts > open contract > select order > push to Shopify","ord":110},
  {"id":"a-payment-was-captured-but-no-order-appeared","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"A payment was captured but no order appeared","a":"<p>Send us the order reference and we will check immediately. The one time this happened in the pilot the order did generate on inspection, so it was a sync delay rather than a lost order, but it is worth flagging every time rather than waiting.</p><p>Before you flag it, check whether the delivery is simply not due yet, since orders are created ahead of the delivery date, not at purchase.</p>","path":"","ord":111},
  {"id":"will-future-orders-be-created-if-the-first-one-was-cancelled","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Will future orders be created if the first one was cancelled?","a":"<p>Not automatically on pay as you go. A cancelled first order does not generate the following ones unless the customer wants the delivery and pays for it. On prepaid, the remaining deliveries are already paid for and continue on schedule unless the subscription itself is cancelled.</p>","path":"","ord":112},
  {"id":"can-you-set-up-free-subscriptions-for-a-competition-or-giveaway","cat":"orders","status":"m","asked":2,"q":"Can you set up free subscriptions for a competition or giveaway?","a":"<p>Yes. We create them manually as paid subscriptions with no money collected, so the winner gets the full experience including the portal and the notifications.</p><p>Send a sheet with the winners’ <strong>email addresses</strong>. Emails only matter if the winners are new: an existing Shopify customer we can find, a brand new one we cannot create without an email.</p>","path":"","ord":113},
  {"id":"can-a-customer-change-their-delivery-frequency-mid-plan","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Can a customer change their delivery frequency mid plan?","a":"<p>Yes, and either side can do it. The customer does it in the portal under their subscriptions and orders. You do it in the app: <strong>Purchase Contracts</strong>, open the subscription, select the delivery and reschedule.</p><p>Real example: a monthly plan with four deliveries left was moved to a two month gap without disturbing what had already shipped.</p>","path":"App > Purchase Contracts > open subscription > Reschedule Delivery","ord":114},
  {"id":"can-you-pause-a-customer-s-subscription-for-a-month","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can you pause a customer’s subscription for a month?","a":"<p>Yes. Send the customer’s email address, or restore our Orders access, and we will shift the deliveries so the next one dispatches the following month. You can also do it yourself from Purchase Contracts, and the customer can do it from the portal.</p>","path":"","ord":115},
  {"id":"can-a-customer-move-to-a-different-pack-size-mid-plan","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can a customer move to a different pack size mid plan?","a":"<p>Yes. The product on the remaining deliveries can be changed, for example from a 10kg to a 5kg pack. On pay as you go we change the product and send a fresh payment link for the new amount. On prepaid the price difference follows the delivery edit rules, so more expensive means a payment link and cheaper means a refund.</p>","path":"","ord":116},
  {"id":"do-the-stackback-products-need-to-go-into-our-wms-or-oms","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Do the StackBack products need to go into our WMS or OMS?","a":"<p>No. The helper products StackBack creates are not shippable and not stock tracked, so they should not be added to Uniware, Easycom or any similar system. Your WMS should only see the child orders, which carry your real products.</p>","path":"","ord":117},
  {"id":"a-subscription-order-shows-as-cancelled-and-we-did-not-cancel-it","cat":"orders","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"A subscription order shows as cancelled and we did not cancel it","a":"<p>Check the order timeline in Shopify first. Every cancellation is stamped there with who did it, and in the pilot these have resolved to an action on the merchant side. Send us the order number and we will pull the timeline with you.</p>","path":"","ord":118},
  {"id":"what-is-the-customer-portal-and-where-does-it-live","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":7,"q":"What is the customer portal and where does it live?","a":"<p>A self service area where your customers manage their own subscriptions. It runs on <strong>your domain</strong>, not ours, so a customer who opens it has not left your site.</p><p>Be clear on what that does and does not mean: the <em>address</em> is yours, the <em>interface</em> is StackBack's. The portal is a hosted template, so it carries our layout with your logo and brand colours applied, not your theme. It is not a page your theme or page builder can restyle.</p><p>Every bundle and subscription page sits under the same path, which is why bundle links look the way they do.</p>","path":"yourdomain.com/apps/stackback-portal","ord":119},
  {"id":"how-do-customers-log-in","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"How do customers log in?","a":"<p>With the same email or phone they used at checkout, and a one time code. No password to create or forget. The session lasts for a day on that device, so a returning customer verifies once per device rather than every visit.</p><p>Codes go by WhatsApp and email. If one channel is not arriving, the other works.</p>","path":"","ord":120},
  {"id":"a-customer-is-not-receiving-the-login-code","cat":"portal","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"A customer is not receiving the login code","a":"<p>Have them use the email route instead. In the cases raised so far the codes were being sent correctly, so the failure was on the delivery side of one channel rather than in the app.</p><p>If a customer is stuck, send us their email and we will confirm from our side whether the code went out.</p>","path":"","ord":121},
  {"id":"what-can-customers-do-themselves","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"What can customers do themselves?","a":"<p>Cancel, reschedule a delivery, pause, resume, swap a product, add a product, view their subscriptions and past orders, see what is due, and pay an outstanding pay as you go invoice.</p><p>Two things they cannot do: issue themselves a refund, and apply an edit that costs more without paying the difference. Both are deliberate.</p>","path":"","ord":122},
  {"id":"can-the-portal-be-linked-from-our-menu-or-an-existing-page","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can the portal be linked from our menu or an existing page?","a":"<p>Both work. A menu entry is the most convenient for customers who want to find their subscription without hunting, and stores have renamed an existing \"Subscribe and Save\" menu item to \"Manage Subscriptions\" pointing at the portal. It can also be embedded from an existing page if you would rather keep the navigation tight.</p>","path":"","ord":123},
  {"id":"can-we-hide-the-manage-subscription-button-after-purchase","cat":"portal","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can we hide the Manage subscription button after purchase?","a":"<p>We would rather you did not, and here is the reason: that button is effectively the only place a customer discovers they can reschedule or manage their own deliveries. Hide it and those requests come to your support inbox instead.</p><p>It also does not take the customer off your site, since the portal is on your domain. If you still want it gone, ask, but weigh the support load first.</p>","path":"","ord":124},
  {"id":"can-we-turn-off-a-portal-action-we-do-not-want-customers-using","cat":"portal","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can we turn off a portal action we do not want customers using?","a":"<p>Yes. Individual portal actions can be disabled per store, and we have done this for the add product flow on request. The setting ships with the next production push rather than instantly, because it needs a deployment.</p>","path":"","ord":125},
  {"id":"can-portal-navigation-be-simplified","cat":"portal","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can portal navigation be simplified?","a":"<p>This was the most consistent portal complaint in the pilot and it is being worked on: subscriptions presented in a grid on the shop tab, and easier movement from a subscription page back to the rest of your site.</p><p>It is a shared update that lands on every live store, so it goes through a testing cycle before rollout and cannot be expedited for one store.</p>","path":"","ord":126},
  {"id":"can-we-add-product-content-and-faqs-to-portal-pages","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can we add product content and FAQs to portal pages?","a":"<p>Yes. Product descriptions, FAQs and promotional cards can be added to portal and bundle pages. The full product page is not reproduced there, so pick the content that actually helps someone decide.</p>","path":"","ord":127},
  {"id":"why-does-checkout-from-the-portal-use-native-shopify-checkout","cat":"portal","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Why does checkout from the portal use native Shopify checkout?","a":"<p>Because the portal and bundle pages are hosted by StackBack, and a third party checkout hooks into your theme rather than our pages. So a purchase started on a portal page finishes on Shopify checkout.</p><p>If everything must go through your checkout partner, use the product page widget, where your checkout is supported, and we will disable the portal purchase pages.</p>","path":"","ord":128},
  {"id":"how-does-cancellation-work","cat":"cancel","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"How does cancellation work?","a":"<p>It is request based, never immediate. The customer presses cancel in the portal and a request lands in the app with the reason they gave. You approve or decline it, so you keep the chance to save the subscription.</p><p>On approval, the amount for the pending deliveries is <strong>calculated for you</strong> and you issue it from that screen. The refund itself runs through Shopify, since Shopify collected the money, so a direct refund goes back to the original payment source exactly like a normal Shopify order refund.</p><p>You choose between store credit and a direct refund. The customer cannot choose that.</p>","path":"","ord":129},
  {"id":"if-a-customer-cancels-early-do-we-recover-the-discount-we-alread","cat":"cancel","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"If a customer cancels early, do we recover the discount we already gave?","a":"<p>No. The discount applied to deliveries that already shipped cannot be recovered. A customer who cancels after two deliveries of a six month plan keeps the six month rate on those two.</p><p>This is the main argument for prepaid over pay as you go, and for putting your freebies later in the plan. Retention workflows are being built, but nothing is in place today, so price the risk in rather than planning to claw it back.</p>","path":"","ord":130},
  {"id":"what-happens-to-unused-prepaid-deliveries","cat":"cancel","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"What happens to unused prepaid deliveries?","a":"<p>They are refunded as part of the cancellation, calculated from the pending deliveries and issued after you approve. Deliveries already shipped are not refunded.</p>","path":"","ord":131},
  {"id":"can-we-handle-cancellations-in-bulk","cat":"cancel","status":"r","asked":3,"q":"Can we handle cancellations in bulk?","a":"<p>Not yet, and we know that reviewing them one by one does not scale. Two things are coming: a preference that lets customers cancel directly without merchant review, and a bulk operations screen for handling requests together, shipping with the reporting module.</p>","path":"","ord":132},
  {"id":"where-does-the-cancellation-policy-show-to-customers","cat":"cancel","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Where does the cancellation policy show to customers?","a":"<p>In the widget, before they subscribe. That is deliberate: a customer who can see the terms at the point of decision raises fewer disputes later.</p>","path":"","ord":133},
  {"id":"can-we-write-our-own-cancellation-policy-text","cat":"cancel","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can we write our own cancellation policy text?","a":"<p>Yes, the policy text is a setting and you write it. Send the copy and we will put it in.</p><p>Stores have also renamed the label, for example to \"Terms and Conditions\", and used the space for the things customers actually ask: cancel any time before dispatch, choose your delivery date before dispatch, switch product mid plan and pay the difference.</p>","path":"App > Settings > cancellation policy text","ord":134},
  {"id":"can-we-block-freebies-and-extra-discounts-on-subscription-orders","cat":"cancel","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we block freebies and extra discounts on subscription orders?","a":"<p>Yes. Automatic gifts and cart discounts can be excluded from subscription lines, so a subscription customer does not collect the subscription rate plus your promotional gift. Ask for it before you launch a promotion, not after.</p>","path":"","ord":135},
  {"id":"we-are-stopping-at-the-end-of-the-pilot-what-happens-to-live-sub","cat":"cancel","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"We are stopping at the end of the pilot. What happens to live subscriptions?","a":"<p>Talk to your account contact before you announce anything to customers, because the answer depends on whether prepaid deliveries are outstanding. Prepaid customers have paid for deliveries you still owe them, so those either run out or get refunded, and that is a commercial decision rather than a switch we flip.</p><p>Your subscription data is exportable, so you are not locked in.</p>","path":"","ord":136},
  {"id":"which-notifications-does-stackback-send","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Which notifications does StackBack send?","a":"<p>Nineteen templates cover the lifecycle. The ones your customers see most:</p><ul><li><strong>Setup</strong>: subscription confirmed, bundle subscription confirmed, one time bundle acknowledged.</li><li><strong>Pay as you go</strong>: first invoice, invoice reminders, payment success.</li><li><strong>Prepaid</strong>: invoice request, payment success.</li><li><strong>Deliveries</strong>: upcoming delivery reminder, delivery order confirmed, reschedule confirmations for a single delivery or all of them, edit summary.</li><li><strong>State changes</strong>: pause confirmed, cancellation request received, prepaid cancellation approved, pay as you go cancellation confirmed.</li><li><strong>AutoPay</strong>: mandate setup invitation, pre debit notice.</li></ul><p>You can preview and test each one from the app before your customers see it.</p>","path":"App > Notification Templates","ord":137},
  {"id":"can-reminders-go-by-whatsapp-instead-of-email","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":9,"q":"Can reminders go by WhatsApp instead of email?","a":"<p>Yes. WhatsApp went live in early September 2026 and pay as you go reminders now go out on WhatsApp as well as email. This matters for stores whose customers do not really read email, which was several of the pilot.</p><p>WhatsApp is enabled per store, so ask your contact to switch it on for yours.</p>","path":"","ord":138},
  {"id":"can-messages-come-from-our-own-whatsapp-number","cat":"notify","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Can messages come from our own WhatsApp number?","a":"<p>Not immediately. Messages currently go through StackBack’s shared WhatsApp business account, which is what lets a customer reach their portal from the message.</p><p>Sending from your own number is possible but needs a WhatsApp Business API integration and Meta app setup, which takes weeks. The shortcut, if you already have a business manager, is to add our three team emails to it, which brings it down to under a week.</p><p>If brand consistency matters more than the channel, several stores turned WhatsApp off and kept email only. That is a one line change.</p>","path":"","ord":139},
  {"id":"can-we-write-our-own-whatsapp-reminder-copy","cat":"notify","status":"n","asked":2,"q":"Can we write our own WhatsApp reminder copy?","a":"<p>Not yet. There is one fixed template per message type and it is not customisable per store, because WhatsApp templates are approved centrally through Meta. Email templates are more flexible, so if the wording matters, use email for that message.</p>","path":"","ord":140},
  {"id":"can-notification-emails-come-from-our-own-domain","cat":"notify","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"Can notification emails come from our own domain?","a":"<p>Possible, but it depends on your email provider and whether it exposes an API we can integrate with. Tell us which provider you use and we will look at it. We cannot commit a timeline without knowing that.</p>","path":"","ord":141},
  {"id":"can-we-change-how-many-days-before-delivery-the-reminder-goes","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Can we change how many days before delivery the reminder goes?","a":"<p>Yes, it is a per store setting. Two numbers matter: <code>time_to_delivery</code>, the lead time before the delivery date (default 7 days), and <code>payg_reminder</code>, how far ahead of that the first invoice goes, with reminders repeating every <code>reminder_gap</code>.</p><p>Widening the reminder window is the fix for customers who feel they were paused without warning, because the pause check itself runs on the delivery date with no grace period.</p>","path":"App > Settings > timing configuration","ord":142},
  {"id":"can-we-keep-a-gap-between-the-reminder-and-the-order-generation-","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we keep a gap between the reminder and the order generation date?","a":"<p>Yes, and one store asked for exactly this so they could confirm the order with the customer by phone before it was processed. Tell us the gap in days and we will configure it.</p>","path":"","ord":143},
  {"id":"a-customer-was-told-their-subscription-was-paused-with-no-warnin","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"A customer was told their subscription was paused with no warning","a":"<p>The invoice and the reminders did go out, but the window was too short for that customer. Reminders now go out with the payment link ahead of the due date, and the timing is yours to widen.</p><p>If you are seeing this repeatedly, increase <code>payg_reminder</code> and add WhatsApp to the channel mix, since email alone reaches fewer customers than you would expect.</p>","path":"","ord":144},
  {"id":"can-whatsapp-reminders-merge-with-our-existing-chat-bot","cat":"notify","status":"n","asked":1,"q":"Can WhatsApp reminders merge with our existing chat bot?","a":"<p>No. Reminders go through Meta directly and cannot be routed through a separate WhatsApp AI product you already run. Your Meta messaging usage is billed to you at the end of the month in the normal way.</p>","path":"","ord":145},
  {"id":"how-do-we-test-the-reminder-flow-without-waiting-a-month","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"How do we test the reminder flow without waiting a month?","a":"<p>Ask us for a test plan option with a very short delivery interval, a few minutes rather than a month. Place an order against it and you will see the invoice, the reminders and the payment success message inside one sitting. Cancel the test order afterwards, it does not need dispatching.</p>","path":"","ord":146},
  {"id":"the-confirmation-says-our-store-name-oddly-or-is-missing-the-sku","cat":"notify","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"The confirmation says our store name oddly, or is missing the SKU","a":"<p>Sender name and message content are adjustable within what the template allows, so send us the exact wording you want. Adding fields the template does not carry, such as a SKU line, is a template change rather than a setting, so it takes longer. Tell us and we will say which one it is.</p>","path":"","ord":147},
  {"id":"how-is-shipping-handled-on-subscriptions","cat":"ship","status":"m","asked":9,"q":"How is shipping handled on subscriptions?","a":"<p>Shipping is configured inside StackBack per store, and it is charged on the child orders, the ones that actually ship, not on the parent order.</p><p>We need your real rate card to set this up: cart value bands, pincode or city zones, weight slabs, whatever you actually use. Send it as a sheet. Until it is configured, shipping is usually set to free as a placeholder, which is not what you want going live.</p>","path":"","ord":148},
  {"id":"our-rates-vary-by-pincode-cart-value-and-weight","cat":"ship","status":"l","asked":5,"q":"Our rates vary by pincode, cart value and weight","a":"<p>All three shapes are configurable, and this is normal for Indian D2C. Send the full matrix and we will build it, then show it back to you in the preview so you can check it against your own sheet before customers see it.</p><p>Flat handling charges can be folded into the rate as well.</p>","path":"","ord":149},
  {"id":"can-we-limit-subscriptions-to-certain-pincodes-or-one-city","cat":"ship","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we limit subscriptions to certain pincodes or one city?","a":"<p>Yes. Stores have launched subscriptions in one city only while they proved out fulfilment. Send the pincode list and subscriptions will only be offered there.</p>","path":"","ord":150},
  {"id":"can-free-shipping-kick-in-above-a-cart-value","cat":"ship","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can free shipping kick in above a cart value?","a":"<p>Yes. Set the threshold and free delivery applies above it. Combined with the quantity selector in the widget, this is a clean way to nudge a bigger basket per delivery.</p>","path":"","ord":151},
  {"id":"shipping-is-being-charged-twice","cat":"ship","status":"m","asked":2,"q":"Shipping is being charged twice","a":"<p>This happens when both StackBack and your checkout partner calculate shipping on the same order. The fix is to switch shipping off on the StackBack side so your existing checkout logic is the only one applying, and we do that for you.</p><p>If you see two shipping lines on an order, send the order number and say which system should own the rate.</p>","path":"","ord":152},
  {"id":"does-the-subscription-use-our-shopify-shipping-profile","cat":"ship","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"Does the subscription use our Shopify shipping profile?","a":"<p>Not automatically. Shipping for subscriptions is configured inside StackBack, so your Shopify profile is not read across. That is why shipping shows as free until we have your rate card.</p>","path":"","ord":153},
  {"id":"do-you-need-markets-access-for-international-shipping","cat":"ship","status":"m","asked":2,"q":"Do you need Markets access for international shipping?","a":"<p>Yes, if you sell internationally. Markets access lets us read your shipping profiles and rates for each market so plans are configured with the right delivery cost. Without it, an international subscription will be priced against domestic assumptions.</p>","path":"","ord":154},
  {"id":"does-taking-a-subscription-reserve-inventory","cat":"stock","status":"n","asked":6,"q":"Does taking a subscription reserve inventory?","a":"<p><strong>No.</strong> Signing up for a subscription does not reserve, block or move stock, in Shopify or in your WMS. Inventory only moves when the delivery order is actually created in Shopify, which is roughly seven days before each delivery date, and from that point it syncs to your WMS like any other order.</p><p>So a customer on a six month plan has not reserved six units. You are committed to six deliveries and holding none of them.</p><p>Correction worth flagging: one pilot store was told on a call that subscriptions do reserve stock through the Shopify to WMS sync. That was wrong and was corrected in writing eight days later. This article is the accurate answer.</p>","path":"","ord":155},
  {"id":"what-happens-if-a-product-is-out-of-stock-on-a-delivery-date","cat":"stock","status":"l","asked":5,"q":"What happens if a product is out of stock on a delivery date?","a":"<p>The delivery order will not be created in Shopify, so nothing ships silently against stock you do not have. What it does mean is that you find out at delivery time rather than in advance.</p><p>How stores handle it today: keep the plan and frequency set deliberately narrow so stock is easy to track, run a manual check ahead of each delivery batch, and if something is short, tell the customer and reschedule the delivery. Orders appear roughly seven days ahead of the delivery date, so that check has a window.</p>","path":"","ord":156},
  {"id":"do-the-stackback-helper-products-affect-our-stock","cat":"stock","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Do the StackBack helper products affect our stock?","a":"<p>No. They are created as not stock tracked and not shippable, so they never decrement inventory. They exist to carry plan pricing through checkout. Do not add them to your WMS.</p>","path":"","ord":157},
  {"id":"freebie-products-are-visible-on-our-website","cat":"stock","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"Freebie products are visible on our website","a":"<p>They should be unlisted, and where this happened it was our configuration error. Tell us and we will correct it. Worth checking your own catalogue after setup, because a visible freebie is a product customers will add to a normal order.</p>","path":"","ord":158},
  {"id":"please-do-not-leave-test-products-active-in-our-catalogue","cat":"stock","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"Please do not leave test products active in our catalogue","a":"<p>Agreed, and this is fair criticism from the pilot. Test and dummy products should be drafted or deleted once they have done their job. If you find one active, tell us and it goes to draft immediately.</p>","path":"","ord":159},
  {"id":"can-we-choose-which-products-get-subscription-products-created","cat":"stock","status":"m","asked":3,"q":"Can we choose which products get subscription products created?","a":"<p>Yes, and you should tell us early. Left unspecified we may configure across a wider set than you intend, and stores have rightly objected to helper products appearing for items they never wanted on subscription. Send the SKU list and we will remove anything created outside it.</p>","path":"","ord":160},
  {"id":"which-checkouts-have-you-worked-with","cat":"integ","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Which checkouts have you worked with?","a":"<p>GoKwik, Shopflo, Shiprocket checkout and Razorpay, plus native Shopify checkout. The product page widget can route through your partner. The landing and bundle pages stay on native Shopify checkout because they are hosted on our portal.</p><p>Coordination with your checkout partner takes time, so bring it up in your first week rather than your launch week.</p>","path":"","ord":161},
  {"id":"which-page-builders-work","cat":"integ","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Which page builders work?","a":"<p>PageFly and GemPages are both in production on pilot stores. On a builder, the placement options for the subscription section are narrower than on a native theme, and builder changes publish straight to your live site, so we name them before making them.</p><p>If your builder supports liquid blocks, product specific landing page links can be passed through dynamically, which makes a per product subscription banner possible.</p>","path":"","ord":162},
  {"id":"can-we-keep-our-loyalty-programme","cat":"integ","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"Can we keep our loyalty programme?","a":"<p>Your loyalty programme keeps running, but be careful about it stacking with subscription discounts. Tell us which programme you use and what it awards, and we will look at whether it needs excluding on subscription lines. Do not assume it composes cleanly.</p>","path":"","ord":163},
  {"id":"do-subscription-orders-flow-into-our-wms-or-oms","cat":"integ","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Do subscription orders flow into our WMS or OMS?","a":"<p>Child orders behave like any other Shopify order, so your existing integration picks them up. Parent orders are auto fulfilled and marked shipping not required, so they should not enter your fulfilment queue. The helper products need no WMS setup.</p><p>If your 3PL is picking up parent orders, tell us, because that means the shipping not required flag is not being honoured somewhere in the chain.</p>","path":"","ord":164},
  {"id":"can-we-export-the-subscription-data-if-we-stop-using-the-app","cat":"integ","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we export the subscription data if we stop using the app?","a":"<p>Yes. Subscription data is exportable and this has been confirmed to several pilot stores. Ask your contact for the export.</p>","path":"","ord":165},
  {"id":"we-saw-stackback-on-another-brand-that-is-not-in-your-pilot","cat":"integ","status":"w","asked":1,"q":"We saw StackBack on another brand that is not in your pilot","a":"<p>Ask us. At least one brand was running a subscription programme that looked similar from the outside while actually operating on spreadsheets with payment links sent by hand. Anything genuinely running on StackBack, we can tell you about.</p>","path":"","ord":166},
  {"id":"our-conversion-rate-dropped-after-launch-is-the-widget-to-blame","cat":"report","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Our conversion rate dropped after launch. Is the widget to blame?","a":"<p>Check your measurement before you change the page, because this has been a false alarm more than once. If general conversion is tracked in your checkout partner’s dashboard while subscriptions complete as Shopify subscription contracts, your product level filters will not count subscription conversions at all, so the same traffic looks like it converted less.</p><p>That said, it is a real risk and stores have seen genuine dips. The two fastest experiments: make one time purchase the default again, and hide the theme buttons while subscribe is selected. Both are reversible within minutes, so test rather than argue.</p>","path":"","ord":167},
  {"id":"how-do-we-see-subscription-performance","cat":"report","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"How do we see subscription performance?","a":"<p>Today, from <strong>Purchase Contracts</strong>, which shows every subscription, its timeline and its state, and from <strong>Payment Reminders</strong> for the pay as you go pipeline. Both export.</p><p>A proper analytics dashboard is on the roadmap and is the most requested item in the pilot. Until it lands, ask your contact for the numbers you want and we will pull them.</p>","path":"App > Purchase Contracts, App > Payment Reminders","ord":168},
  {"id":"how-do-we-know-who-cancelled-an-autopay-mandate","cat":"report","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"How do we know who cancelled an AutoPay mandate?","a":"<p>Purchase Contracts shows it immediately: a customer whose mandate is cancelled shows as downgraded there. A dedicated view is part of the analytics work still to come.</p>","path":"","ord":169},
  {"id":"will-the-parent-orders-distort-our-analytics","cat":"report","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Will the parent orders distort our analytics?","a":"<p>They will show as paid orders, so filter them out. They carry a helper line item that is not stock tracked, so inventory and true revenue are unaffected, but an unfiltered order count will double. Ask us for the filter for your store.</p>","path":"","ord":170},
  {"id":"what-did-the-store-data-analysis-actually-tell-you","cat":"report","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"What did the store data analysis actually tell you?","a":"<p>The number that drives the plan proposal is the <strong>gap between repeat purchases</strong>. Real pilot outputs: 28 to 35 days led to monthly delivery on 3, 6 and 12 month plans; 12 to 18 days led to a fortnightly plan; a 45.9 percent repeat rate with a 7 day gap led to weekly.</p><p>We also look at repeat customer share, order volume, average order value and which SKUs actually repeat, which is how the subscription SKU shortlist gets built. Ask for the sheet, it is yours.</p>","path":"","ord":171},
  {"id":"our-gst-report-and-our-sales-dashboard-do-not-agree","cat":"report","status":"l","asked":2,"q":"Our GST report and our sales dashboard do not agree","a":"<p>That gap is in your Shopify and accounting reporting rather than in StackBack, and at least one store found a difference in the lakhs. Two things to check that are subscription related: whether parent orders are being counted twice, and whether prepaid revenue is being recognised at collection or at delivery. Tell us what you find, because it affects how we advise the next store.</p>","path":"","ord":172},
  {"id":"is-the-pilot-really-free-shopify-shows-a-charge","cat":"bill","status":"w","asked":7,"q":"Is the pilot really free? Shopify shows a charge.","a":"<p>The pilot is free. Shopify requires an app to declare a price in its billing setup, so a figure appears on the install screen for compliance reasons. Pilot and grace windows keep pilot stores unbilled.</p><p>If a charge ever actually appears on your Shopify invoice, tell us immediately, that would be an error.</p>","path":"","ord":173},
  {"id":"what-will-we-pay-after-the-pilot","cat":"bill","status":"l","asked":6,"q":"What will we pay after the pilot?","a":"<p>That is a commercial conversation with your account owner, not something to read off a table here, and it is deliberately being shaped from what the pilot actually shows about your volumes. Stores have asked for a flexible plan built from their pilot data and that is the approach.</p><p>What we can say now: the shape is a tier based subscription plan with a subscription volume limit per tier. Ask your account contact for numbers in writing.</p>","path":"","ord":174},
  {"id":"what-are-the-plan-tiers-and-what-does-each-include","cat":"bill","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"What are the plan tiers and what does each include?","a":"<p>Five tiers, differing in how many active subscriptions are included and which page templates you get:</p><ul><li><strong>Free</strong>: no subscriptions. Widget and customer portal templates only.</li><li><strong>Starter</strong>: up to 5 subscriptions. Widget and portal.</li><li><strong>Growth</strong>: up to 20. Adds the subscription landing page and bundle builder templates.</li><li><strong>Pro</strong>: up to 250. Adds the flash sale landing page.</li><li><strong>Scale</strong>: unlimited subscriptions, all templates.</li></ul><p>Verified against the build on 5 September 2026. Any older four tier dollar pricing you may have seen is out of date and should not be quoted.</p>","path":"","ord":175},
  {"id":"how-is-the-subscription-limit-counted","cat":"bill","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"How is the subscription limit counted?","a":"<p>By active subscriptions on the plan, not by orders, so one customer on a twelve month plan counts once rather than twelve times. Usage is tracked as subscriptions are created, and going over the included count is what drives an overage.</p>","path":"","ord":176},
  {"id":"who-do-we-talk-to-about-commercials","cat":"bill","status":"m","asked":2,"q":"Who do we talk to about commercials?","a":"<p>Your account owner, not the activation team. Anything about pricing after the pilot, contract terms or paperwork should be in writing by email. The activation group is the right place for configuration, bugs and go live coordination.</p>","path":"","ord":177},
  {"id":"pre-go-live-checklist","cat":"start","status":"m","asked":12,"q":"Pre go-live checklist","a":"<p>Nine things worth confirming before the widget touches your live theme. Most of the pilot incidents traced back to one of these being skipped.</p><ol><li><strong>COD disabled</strong> on subscription products, in your checkout partner as well as Shopify.</li><li><strong>Shipping rates configured</strong> in the app. If they still read as free, they are a placeholder, not a decision.</li><li><strong>Automatic cart discounts excluded</strong> from subscription lines, so plan discounts do not stack into a 35 percent order.</li><li><strong>Freebie products unlisted</strong>, so customers cannot buy them as normal items.</li><li><strong>Test and dummy products drafted or deleted</strong>.</li><li><strong>Plans assigned to every variant</strong> you expect the widget on. A variant with no plan shows no widget.</li><li><strong>Cancellation policy text</strong> written in your words, since it shows to customers before they subscribe.</li><li><strong>One real test order placed</strong> on a live unlisted product, and its parent and child orders inspected.</li><li><strong>Your ops team briefed</strong> that a subscription creates two orders and only the child order ships.</li></ol><p>Ask us to walk the list with you on the go live call rather than reading it alone.</p>","path":"","ord":178},
  {"id":"the-words-we-use-translated","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"The words we use, translated","a":"<p>The app borrows some Shopify vocabulary and invents a little of its own. This is the whole glossary.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase contract</strong>: one customer's subscription. The record holding the plan, the customer and every scheduled delivery. This is where you go to act on a single customer.</li><li><strong>Timeline</strong>: the list of deliveries inside a contract, one row per delivery, each with its own date and status.</li><li><strong>Delivery</strong>: one shipment in the plan. Not the same thing as an order until it is pushed to Shopify.</li><li><strong>Parent order</strong>: the Shopify order that collects the subscription payment and holds the contract. Auto fulfilled, never shipped.</li><li><strong>Child order</strong>: the real Shopify order for one delivery. This is the one that ships.</li><li><strong>Purchase options</strong>: the tabs on the product page, one time, subscribe and save, bundle and save.</li><li><strong>Plan option</strong>: one card inside the subscribe tab, for example 3 months with monthly delivery at 12 percent.</li><li><strong>Selling plan</strong>: Shopify's own name for the same thing, which is what you will see if you look in your Shopify admin.</li><li><strong>Fixed bundle</strong>: a combination you define. <strong>Build your own box</strong>: a pool the customer picks from. Internally the second is called mix and match, so you may see that label.</li><li><strong>Custom Checkout Adapt</strong>: the mechanism that lets subscriptions run through a third party checkout. It creates the helper plan products in your catalogue.</li><li><strong>Mandate</strong>: a customer's standing UPI AutoPay authorisation held at Razorpay.</li><li><strong>PAYG</strong>: pay as you go.</li></ul>","path":"","ord":179},
  {"id":"what-the-pilot-includes-and-what-we-need-from-you","cat":"start","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"What the pilot includes, and what we need from you","a":"<p>The pilot is zero cost and covers both the platform and the consultation. We do the hands on work: reading your order data, proposing the plans, configuring subscriptions and bundles, setting up the widget, the landing pages and the customer portal, and running the go live. You are not expected to build anything.</p><p>What we need in return is decisions. Your business context, your margin limits, and a yes or no on the plan proposals. The stores that moved fastest answered the plan sheet within a couple of days. The stores that stalled, stalled on the collaborator code or on their own theme launch, not on us.</p>","path":"","ord":180},
  {"id":"what-each-page-in-the-app-is-for","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":8,"q":"What each page in the app is for","a":"<p>The left hand nav in the Shopify admin app, in order, and what you actually use each one for:</p><ul><li><strong>Home</strong>: overview.</li><li><strong>Manage Purchase Options</strong>: your plans and the widget settings that go with them.</li><li><strong>Purchase Contracts</strong>: every subscription, its timeline and its state. The page you will live in. Reschedule, pause, push an order early, see who cancelled a mandate.</li><li><strong>One-Time Orders</strong>: one time bundle purchases, which are not subscriptions and have no timeline.</li><li><strong>Orders</strong>: the orders StackBack has created.</li><li><strong>Payment Reminders</strong>: the pay as you go invoice pipeline, who has been invoiced and who has paid. Exports.</li><li><strong>Customers</strong>: subscribers and their contracts.</li><li><strong>Notification Templates</strong>: all 19 messages, with preview and test send.</li><li><strong>Portal Settings</strong>: the customer portal look and which actions are enabled.</li><li><strong>Landing Pages</strong>: the subscription, bundle builder and flash sale pages and their customisation.</li><li><strong>Widgets</strong>: the product page widget template.</li><li><strong>Custom Checkout Adapt</strong>: third party checkout support and the helper plan products it creates.</li><li><strong>Integrations</strong>: WhatsApp and other connections.</li><li><strong>AutoPay</strong> and <strong>AutoPay Jobs</strong>: only visible once AutoPay is switched on for your store.</li><li><strong>Billing</strong>: only visible once billing is switched on, so pilot stores will not see it.</li><li><strong>Settings</strong>: order placement, timing, cancellation policy, brand, shipping zones.</li></ul><p>If a page you were told about is missing, it is gated on your store rather than broken. Ask.</p>","path":"","ord":181},
  {"id":"what-you-can-change-yourself-in-settings","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":7,"q":"What you can change yourself in Settings","a":"<p>Five tabs, and everything in them is yours to change without asking us:</p><ul><li><strong>Order Placement</strong>: whether delivery orders are created individually as they come due, or batched at a set time of day, plus that time and your timezone.</li><li><strong>Timing Configurations</strong>: the lead time before a delivery, how far ahead invoices and reminders go out, the gap between reminders, and AutoPay retry attempts.</li><li><strong>Cancellation Policy</strong>: the text customers see before they subscribe.</li><li><strong>Brand Settings</strong>: portal logo, primary and secondary colour.</li><li><strong>Shipping Zones</strong>: sync your Shopify zones into the app.</li></ul><p>Batch mode is worth knowing about. If your warehouse works to a cut off, set batch placement to a fixed time and every delivery order for that day lands together instead of trickling in.</p>","path":"App > Settings","ord":182},
  {"id":"can-we-add-or-remove-products-from-a-plan-ourselves","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Can we add or remove products from a plan ourselves?","a":"<p>Yes. Adding and removing products on existing plans is a merchant action and you do not need us for it. Do tell us when you add a <strong>new variant</strong> to a product that already has plans, because a new variant does not inherit the plan and will show no widget until it is attached.</p>","path":"App > Manage Purchase Options","ord":183},
  {"id":"where-do-i-see-a-subscription-s-schedule-and-status","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":6,"q":"Where do I see a subscription's schedule and status?","a":"<p>Purchase Contracts, then open the contract. You get the plan, the customer, and the full delivery timeline: which deliveries are paid, which have been pushed to Shopify with their order number linked through, and which are still scheduled.</p><p>Your Shopify Orders list will not tell you this. It only shows finished orders, which is the right view for your fulfilment team and the wrong one for answering a customer question.</p>","path":"App > Purchase Contracts > open a contract","ord":184},
  {"id":"how-do-we-identify-subscription-orders-inside-shopify","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"How do we identify subscription orders inside Shopify?","a":"<p>By tag. Every order StackBack creates is tagged, so you can filter and segment on it in Shopify and in your reporting:</p><ul><li>Subscription orders carry a tag in the form <code>subscription-id-&lt;number&gt;</code>, where the number is the contract, so every delivery from one customer's plan shares it.</li><li>AutoPay orders additionally carry <code>subscription</code>, <code>autopay</code> and <code>razorpay</code>.</li></ul><p>This is also the cleanest way to filter parent orders out of a revenue report.</p>","path":"","ord":185},
  {"id":"can-we-resequence-the-purchase-option-tabs","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can we resequence the purchase option tabs?","a":"<p>Yes, and it is a live change in the app rather than a development request. If bundle and save is the tab you want customers to see first, it can be first. Stores have reordered these on a call while watching the page.</p>","path":"","ord":186},
  {"id":"where-do-cancellation-requests-appear-and-will-you-email-us","cat":"admin","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Where do cancellation requests appear, and will you email us?","a":"<p>They appear in the app, under Notifications and against the contract in Purchase Contracts. <strong>There is no merchant email alert yet</strong>, so for now someone on your side needs to check the app, and our team also tracks requests and flags them to you in your store group.</p><p>Merchant email notification is planned. Until it ships, agree a rhythm with your team: a daily check is enough for most stores at pilot volumes.</p>","path":"App > Notifications, or App > Purchase Contracts","ord":187},
  {"id":"the-app-was-briefly-unavailable-or-showed-updates-running","cat":"admin","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"The app was briefly unavailable, or showed updates running","a":"<p>Production deployments take the app down for a short window, and because stores share the deployment they land for everyone at once. We push these overnight for exactly that reason.</p><p>Two consequences worth planning around: a setting change that needs a code deployment goes out in the next push rather than immediately, and we will not deploy mid day to fix one store's cosmetic request. Configuration changes are instant and are not affected by any of this.</p>","path":"","ord":188},
  {"id":"how-to-raise-an-issue-so-it-gets-fixed-fast","cat":"admin","status":"m","asked":6,"q":"How to raise an issue so it gets fixed fast","a":"<p>Send it in your store group with these four things, and most issues are diagnosed on the first reply:</p><ol><li><strong>Did you check in incognito with a hard refresh?</strong> Say so. Caching explains a large share of reports.</li><li><strong>The order number or the customer email</strong>, if it is about a specific subscription. Without it we cannot look.</li><li><strong>A screenshot or, better, a screen recording.</strong> For anything intermittent at checkout, a recording is the difference between a fix and a week of guessing.</li><li><strong>Whether anyone on your side changed anything.</strong> Theme edits, a new app, a discount, a config change in StackBack. Several incidents resolved to a change on the store side, and saying so early is not an admission, it is a shortcut.</li></ol><p>Also tell us if you need our Orders access restored. We cannot action customer requests without it.</p>","path":"","ord":189},
  {"id":"is-there-an-inventory-forecast-report","cat":"admin","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Is there an inventory forecast report?","a":"<p>Not today. A forecast tab showing upcoming delivery requirements against current stock was discussed with pilot stores in July 2026 and is not in the build, so do not plan your purchasing around it. Stock checking is manual, and the practical window is the roughly seven days between an order being created and its delivery date.</p><p>What you can do now: filter Purchase Contracts by upcoming delivery dates to see what is coming, and keep plan durations short so you are never planning stock more than a month or two out.</p>","path":"","ord":190},
  {"id":"can-stock-or-cancellation-alerts-go-to-our-warehouse-manager-ins","cat":"admin","status":"n","asked":2,"q":"Can stock or cancellation alerts go to our warehouse manager instead?","a":"<p>Not yet. Alert recipients are not configurable per notification type, so app notifications go to whoever is in the app. Route them internally for now, and tell us if this is blocking, because recipient control has been asked for by more than one store.</p>","path":"","ord":191},
  {"id":"what-happens-to-live-subscriptions-if-we-uninstall-the-app","cat":"admin","status":"x","asked":4,"q":"What happens to live subscriptions if we uninstall the app?","a":"<p><strong>Uninstalling cancels every subscription on the store.</strong> Not pauses, cancels. The app also clears the store's queued jobs, so nothing further is created or charged, and the session is removed.</p><p>This matters most if you have <strong>prepaid</strong> customers. They have paid for deliveries you still owe them, and uninstalling ends their subscriptions without refunding them. Those refunds are yours to issue in Shopify.</p><p>So do not uninstall as a way of pausing, and do not uninstall to test something. If you want to stop new subscriptions, hide the widget instead, which takes minutes and is reversible. If you are genuinely leaving, talk to your account owner first, take the data export, and settle the prepaid balances deliberately.</p>","path":"","ord":192},
  {"id":"what-happens-to-customer-data-on-uninstall-or-a-data-request","cat":"admin","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"What happens to customer data on uninstall or a data request?","a":"<p>Uninstall triggers a cleanup and is logged as a compliance action. Shopify's GDPR webhooks are implemented, so a customer data request and a customer or shop erase request are honoured through the standard Shopify flow, and there is a status page for deletion requests.</p><p>If a customer contacts you directly with a deletion request, route it through Shopify rather than asking us to delete records by hand, so it is logged properly.</p>","path":"","ord":193},
  {"id":"can-a-plan-card-carry-its-own-name-or-tagline","cat":"plans","status":"n","asked":5,"q":"Can a plan card carry its own name or tagline?","a":"<p>Not today. Card content is generated from the duration and frequency you configure, so \"3 months, best for beginners\" or \"recommended by doctors\" cannot be written onto a card.</p><p>The one editable space is the <strong>per delivery label</strong> on each card, which can be repurposed as free text instead of showing the per delivery price. We would push back on doing that: the per delivery price is the strongest number on the widget, because it is what a customer compares against your one time pack price.</p>","path":"","ord":194},
  {"id":"can-the-widget-show-a-struck-through-original-price","cat":"plans","status":"n","asked":4,"q":"Can the widget show a struck-through original price?","a":"<p>Not on the widget. Compare at pricing shows on your theme's own price block, but the plan cards show the discounted per delivery price and the saving rather than a struck through original.</p><p>What works instead: show the saving in rupees rather than a percentage, which several stores switched to, and label the per delivery price clearly so the comparison is obvious.</p>","path":"","ord":195},
  {"id":"customer-chosen-delivery-counts-or-fixed-plan-cards","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":6,"q":"Customer-chosen delivery counts, or fixed plan cards?","a":"<p>Use fixed plan cards. This is the most consequential plan decision and it is easy to get wrong.</p><p>A <strong>customer choice</strong> card lets the shopper pick how many deliveries they want. It looks flexible, and it costs you both of your best levers:</p><ul><li><strong>No freebies.</strong> Freebies attach to a specific delivery number, which only exists on a preconfigured plan.</li><li><strong>No tiered discount.</strong> The rate stays flat however many deliveries they choose, so a twelve delivery commitment earns the same as three.</li></ul><p>Preconfigured cards give each duration its own rate and its own freebie ladder, which is what actually moves customers up the plan. If you do use customer choice, you can at least set a minimum delivery count.</p>","path":"","ord":196},
  {"id":"can-we-run-a-buy-one-get-one-offer","cat":"plans","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Can we run a buy one get one offer?","a":"<p>Not as a buy one get one mechanic. The equivalent is configured as a percentage: a store wanting one free with one bought went live at 55 percent, which is the same economics with clearer maths on the widget.</p><p>Freebies are the closer fit if you want a specific product given away, since they attach to a delivery and appear as a gift rather than as a discount.</p>","path":"","ord":197},
  {"id":"our-variants-have-different-consumption-rates-can-plans-reflect-","cat":"plans","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Our variants have different consumption rates. Can plans reflect that?","a":"<p>Yes. Plans can be aligned to consumption, so a variant a customer gets through twice as fast is configured with the quantity and price that matches. A three month plan then means three months of actual supply on both variants rather than three deliveries of the same pack.</p><p>Get the per delivery price and a plain \"one delivery every month\" line onto the cards when you do this, otherwise the customer cannot tell why two plans of the same name cost different amounts.</p>","path":"","ord":198},
  {"id":"your-suggested-discount-is-more-than-our-margins-allow","cat":"plans","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"Your suggested discount is more than our margins allow","a":"<p>Say so and we will rework it. Stores have capped us at 10 percent once Shopify fees, gateway fees and performance marketing were stacked on the reduced price, and that is a legitimate ceiling.</p><p>The restructure that worked: move your <strong>sitewide checkout discount to an absolute rupee value</strong> (200 or 500 off) and keep percentage discounts for subscriptions and combos. That stops the two discounts compounding on the same order and leaves room for the subscription rate to be the better offer. Send us your SKUs, prices and order frequencies and we will come back with flat rates rather than a percentage band.</p>","path":"","ord":199},
  {"id":"our-theme-applies-a-permanent-discount-so-the-subscription-price","cat":"plans","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Our theme applies a permanent discount, so the subscription price looks higher than our live price","a":"<p>This is the hardest pricing case in the pilot and it has a specific fix. A discount applied by a theme setting or a cart level code is <strong>not visible to us through the Shopify API</strong>, so the widget prices off the Shopify product price and lands above your effective live price. The widget is not wrong, it cannot see the discount.</p><p>What we do not do is rewrite your price or compare at price to make the maths work, because in India your <strong>MRP is a legal value</strong>. Warehousing partners pull product data from Shopify and check the MRP against the physical pack label, and a mismatch attracts a metrology notice. One store was mid relabelling and carrying a future MRP in Shopify for exactly that reason.</p><p>The approach that worked: ignore the theme discount percentage, define the subscription discount as an <strong>absolute amount off MRP</strong> for each plan length, let us compute the per delivery price from that, and show the extra saving as a line in the cart when a subscription is added. Your existing one time discount flow stays untouched.</p>","path":"","ord":200},
  {"id":"pack-sizes-should-be-shopify-variants-not-bundles","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":5,"q":"Pack sizes should be Shopify variants, not bundles","a":"<p>If what you want is a pack of one, pack of two, pack of three layout with the saving shown on each, build those as <strong>Shopify variants</strong>, one per pack size, and layer subscribe and save or bundle and save on top. Do not build them as bundles.</p><p>Bundles are for combinations of different products. A fixed bundle per pack size gets visually heavy once several are stacked on one page, and it splits data that belongs on one product.</p>","path":"","ord":201},
  {"id":"can-we-nest-sub-categories-inside-one-bundle","cat":"bundles","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can we nest sub-categories inside one bundle?","a":"<p>Not inside a bundle. A pregnancy bundle containing separate body care and face care groups cannot be built as one nested bundle, and it cannot be done store side with metafields either, because the widget is an app rather than theme code. It is on the roadmap.</p><p>The workaround is the <strong>Flash Sale page</strong>, which does support a hierarchy: several bundles presented inside one page, with titles you rename. It was built for timed promotions, so ask us about the countdown before you use it as a permanent category page.</p>","path":"","ord":202},
  {"id":"what-is-the-flash-sale-landing-page","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"What is the Flash Sale landing page?","a":"<p>A landing page template that presents several bundles together on one page under headings you control. It exists for timed promotions, and it is the only surface today that gives you a grouped, multi bundle page rather than one bundle per page.</p><p>It is available on the higher plan tiers. If you want it as a permanent category style page rather than a promotion, tell us, because the timer is designed to be part of it.</p>","path":"App > Landing Pages > Flash Sale","ord":203},
  {"id":"can-original-and-discounted-prices-show-per-product-inside-a-bun","cat":"bundles","status":"r","asked":3,"q":"Can original and discounted prices show per product inside a bundle?","a":"<p>Not yet. The price and saving label exists on subscription plan cards but not against individual products inside a bundle, so the customer sees the bundle level discount and not the per item saving. It is on the roadmap.</p>","path":"","ord":204},
  {"id":"can-the-bundle-discount-tiers-and-progress-bar-be-changed","cat":"bundles","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can the bundle discount tiers and progress bar be changed?","a":"<p>Yes, and the tiers are live edits. Thresholds have been reconfigured on calls while the merchant watched, for example two items at 10 percent, four at 12, six at 15.</p><p>Check the progress bar is actually visible on your theme. On more than one store it rendered in a grey that customers would never notice, and recolouring it to something high contrast is a quick change. A bar nobody sees is not a nudge.</p>","path":"","ord":205},
  {"id":"how-much-of-the-widget-layout-can-we-change","cat":"widget","status":"n","asked":9,"q":"How much of the widget layout can we change?","a":"<p>Colours and text, yes. <strong>Layout, no.</strong> Element positions cannot be moved, a discount notch cannot be shifted from the right to the centre, borders cannot be thickened, and the widget does not accept custom CSS.</p><p>The reason is worth knowing so you can stop asking for the impossible and start asking for the possible: the widget is a React application that StackBack renders inside a Shopify theme block. The block is not editable theme code, and the layout is shared, so a layout change is a change for every store running the widget. That is why some copy and layout requests come back as \"weeks, after testing across stores\" rather than \"this afternoon\".</p><p>What you can change: colours, corner radius, font size, label text where the field exists, which elements and tabs are shown, tab order, and placement above or below the buttons.</p>","path":"","ord":206},
  {"id":"can-our-developer-integrate-the-widget-through-your-api","cat":"widget","status":"n","asked":4,"q":"Can our developer integrate the widget through your API?","a":"<p>No. There is no public API for a third party developer to build against, and we do not give backend access to the app. This has been asked by several stores with capable in house teams and the answer has not changed.</p><p>The route that works: send us your design and we build it. Additional widget templates are in the pipeline, and once that is in place a genuinely different design can be developed to your spec rather than approximated with CSS.</p>","path":"","ord":207},
  {"id":"can-the-widget-merge-into-our-variant-or-pack-selector","cat":"widget","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Can the widget merge into our variant or pack selector?","a":"<p>No. The widget and your theme's variant block are two separate blocks and cannot be combined into one control.</p><p>Three layouts that do work, and one of them usually solves the underlying problem: keep one time as the primary tab so the page opens simple, remove the widget's own pack tabs and quantity selector and sit the plan cards directly below your variant selector, or move subscriptions onto the landing page if your product page is already crowded.</p>","path":"","ord":208},
  {"id":"our-theme-agency-is-editing-the-theme-in-parallel-is-that-a-prob","cat":"widget","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Our theme agency is editing the theme in parallel. Is that a problem?","a":"<p>No. Let them work on their theme, and we re-integrate our changes once their version is live. We would rather redo the placement than have two teams editing one theme at the same time.</p><p>Just tell us when they publish, because a published theme without the re-integration means the widget disappears from your product pages.</p>","path":"","ord":209},
  {"id":"if-a-customer-applies-a-coupon-at-checkout-does-it-apply-to-ever","cat":"pay","status":"l","asked":5,"q":"If a customer applies a coupon at checkout, does it apply to every future delivery?","a":"<p>No. A coupon applies only to the payment being made at that checkout, which is the first order. Later deliveries are charged at the configured plan amount.</p><p>That sounds safe and it is a support problem waiting to happen, because the customer believes they have locked in a rate. Exclude subscription products from discount codes and put the incentive into the plan discount instead, where the customer sees it on the widget and it holds for the whole plan.</p>","path":"","ord":210},
  {"id":"will-our-payment-method-offer-apply-to-subscription-products","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"Will our payment-method offer apply to subscription products?","a":"<p>Yes, unless you exclude them. A UPI or card offer configured in your checkout, for example 49 rupees off, will show on subscription products too and stack on the plan discount.</p><p>Ask us for the list of subscription product and variant IDs, they are unlisted in your Shopify backend, and exclude them on your checkout provider's side. This is the same exclusion list you need for COD, so do both at once.</p>","path":"","ord":211},
  {"id":"how-do-we-test-that-the-second-and-third-orders-actually-get-cre","cat":"pay","status":"m","asked":4,"q":"How do we test that the second and third orders actually get created?","a":"<p>Place a real subscription using a test gateway rather than waiting a month:</p><ul><li>Shopify's <strong>dummy payment gateway</strong>, or</li><li>your checkout provider's <strong>sandbox mode</strong>, if they have one.</li></ul><p>Then ask us for a plan option with a very short delivery interval so the second and third deliveries come due within the same sitting. That gives you the full sequence, order creation, invoice, reminder and payment, before a real customer sees it. We will help set it up.</p>","path":"","ord":212},
  {"id":"on-a-prepaid-plan-where-does-the-money-sit-across-the-deliveries","cat":"pay","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"On a prepaid plan, where does the money sit across the deliveries?","a":"<p>The full amount is collected at the first checkout and then adjusted against each delivery order as it is created over the plan. So the revenue lands with you upfront and each child order draws against it rather than charging again.</p><p>Two implications: <strong>shipping has to be estimated and included upfront</strong>, because there is nothing to collect later, and every delivery in the plan is already marked paid, which is why prepaid customers get a heads up reminder rather than a payment link.</p>","path":"","ord":213},
  {"id":"why-did-you-point-subscriptions-at-shopify-checkout-instead-of-o","cat":"pay","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"Why did you point subscriptions at Shopify checkout instead of our fast checkout?","a":"<p>Reliability of the recurring payment link. A third party checkout that is not natively integrated with the subscription flow tends to skip steps or throw errors that are very hard to trace, and a broken payment link means a delivery that never ships.</p><p>So we start on Shopify checkout, then move the product page widget onto your checkout once it is verified. Your fast checkout is supported on the widget flow. The hosted landing and bundle pages stay on Shopify checkout, and if fast checkout everywhere is a hard requirement, say so early: it is the one constraint that has changed launch plans in the pilot.</p>","path":"","ord":214},
  {"id":"can-two-subscriptions-from-one-customer-ship-as-a-single-order","cat":"orders","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Can two subscriptions from one customer ship as a single order?","a":"<p>Not today. Each subscription creates its own order, so a customer with two plans falling on the same date generates two orders and you pick, pack and ship twice.</p><p>It is on the roadmap. If it is costing you real money, tell us how often it happens, because volume is what moves it up the list. The interim options are nudging the customer into one plan with more items per delivery, or combining the two shipments at your warehouse against the shared customer.</p>","path":"","ord":215},
  {"id":"can-stackback-replace-a-manual-subscription-setup-we-already-run","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can StackBack replace a manual subscription setup we already run?","a":"<p>Yes, and more than one pilot store came off spreadsheets and hand sent payment links. We configure everything on a copy of your theme, you review it on a preview, and your existing manual subscription section is removed or hidden at the point of activation so there is only one path live.</p><p>Tell us where the manual flow lives on your site, including any menu items and landing pages, so nothing is left behind pointing customers at the old process.</p>","path":"","ord":216},
  {"id":"can-stackback-create-a-new-product-for-us-like-a-monthly-surpris","cat":"orders","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Can StackBack create a new product for us, like a monthly surprise box?","a":"<p>Yes, and this is a good use of a subscription. We have built a curated box product with variants, a description explaining the curation, images from your existing assets and a price derived from your own SKU pricing. It lands in your Shopify products, it is yours, and you can edit it.</p><p>Two things to know: one variant is preselected by default and which one can be changed on request, and the product stays unlisted until you approve it, so it will not appear on your site or in the portal before then.</p>","path":"","ord":217},
  {"id":"what-do-swap-add-and-reschedule-actually-do-and-who-pays","cat":"portal","status":"l","asked":6,"q":"What do swap, add and reschedule actually do, and who pays?","a":"<p>The mechanics matter, because customers describe all three as \"changing my subscription\".</p><ul><li><strong>Swap</strong>: replaces the product on the <strong>next delivery only</strong>, not the whole plan. From the portal the customer can swap to something of the same or higher value, and they must pay the difference before it takes effect. A swap to something cheaper is a refund, so only you can do that.</li><li><strong>Add</strong>: same rule. The extra item is only added once paid for, as a one time item on that delivery, not as part of the subscription.</li><li><strong>Reschedule</strong>: no payment. The customer either pushes the next delivery to a later date, or shifts every remaining delivery by a set number of days. Works on prepaid and pay as you go.</li></ul><p>The trap: until the payment lands, the delivery still holds the old basket. Check the delivery in Purchase Contracts before you pick and pack.</p>","path":"","ord":218},
  {"id":"can-a-customer-skip-just-one-delivery","cat":"portal","status":"n","asked":3,"q":"Can a customer skip just one delivery?","a":"<p>No, a single delivery cannot be cancelled out of a plan. What they can do gets to the same place:</p><ul><li><strong>Already paid for</strong> (prepaid, or a paid pay as you go delivery): reschedule, which pushes that delivery and everything after it out. They still receive every delivery they paid for, just later.</li><li><strong>Not yet paid</strong> (pay as you go): they simply do not pay it. The subscription pauses and picks up from that point when they pay.</li></ul><p>\"I am travelling next month\" is a reschedule, and the customer can do it themselves.</p>","path":"","ord":219},
  {"id":"can-a-customer-add-a-one-time-item-to-an-upcoming-delivery","cat":"portal","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can a customer add a one-time item to an upcoming delivery?","a":"<p>Yes, from the portal, as a one time add on paid for separately. It cannot be added as a subscription item.</p><p>There is a cutoff: the item has to be added before the delivery order is created in Shopify, so with a seven day lead time the practical deadline is about eight days before the delivery date. After the order exists, it is a normal Shopify order and you handle it as one.</p>","path":"","ord":220},
  {"id":"can-a-customer-pay-for-their-next-delivery-early","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"Can a customer pay for their next delivery early?","a":"<p>Yes. The portal shows paid and unpaid deliveries separately and a pay as you go customer can settle the next one without waiting for the reminder. Useful for customers who want to get ahead before travelling.</p>","path":"","ord":221},
  {"id":"does-the-portal-show-one-time-orders-too","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Does the portal show one-time orders too?","a":"<p>Yes. Under their subscriptions and orders a customer sees both their subscriptions with delivery schedules and the single orders they placed. Their normal Shopify order history is unaffected and still works.</p><p>The portal also has a shop section where you can surface selected bundles and subscriptions for a logged in customer to buy, and a block that can show a discount code to logged in customers. Ask if you want either enabled.</p>","path":"","ord":222},
  {"id":"does-a-customer-have-to-log-in-every-time","cat":"portal","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"Does a customer have to log in every time?","a":"<p>No. They verify once with a code, and the session is kept in a cookie on that device, so they come back into the portal without re-verifying. A new device or a cleared browser means one more code.</p><p>Being logged into your Shopify customer account does not carry across. The portal login is its own step.</p>","path":"","ord":223},
  {"id":"can-we-decline-a-cancellation-request","cat":"cancel","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can we decline a cancellation request?","a":"<p>Yes. The request comes to you with the reason the customer gave, and you can decline it and keep the subscription running. That is the point of it being request based rather than instant.</p><p>Use it deliberately rather than reflexively. A declined cancellation on a prepaid plan with deliveries still owed is a customer you will hear from again, and there is no retention workflow in the app yet to soften it.</p>","path":"","ord":224},
  {"id":"what-does-a-prepaid-customer-receive-before-each-delivery","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"What does a prepaid customer receive before each delivery?","a":"<p>A heads up, not an invoice. Every delivery on a prepaid plan is already paid, so the customer gets a reminder ahead of each one telling them it is coming, roughly ten days out, and no payment link.</p><p>This is a genuinely useful message and worth keeping on. A prepaid customer who forgot they subscribed and gets a surprise parcel is a refund request.</p>","path":"","ord":225},
  {"id":"how-is-the-payment-reminder-date-actually-derived","cat":"notify","status":"w","asked":3,"q":"How is the payment reminder date actually derived?","a":"<p>Backwards from your processing time, not from a fixed calendar rule. If a delivery is due on the 10th and your lead time is five days, the order has to exist in Shopify by the 5th, so the customer has to have paid before the 5th, so the first payment link goes out around the 1st. Reminders then repeat at the interval you set until they pay.</p><p>That is why changing your lead time also moves your invoice dates. If you widen the warehouse runway, check the reminder window still gives the customer enough time.</p>","path":"App > Settings > Timing Configurations","ord":226},
  {"id":"is-sms-available","cat":"notify","status":"r","asked":3,"q":"Is SMS available?","a":"<p>Not yet. Email is live, WhatsApp is live, SMS is on the roadmap. Worth knowing why it is on the list: over the pilot period SMS has been converting better than WhatsApp for payment reminders, because WhatsApp promotional clutter has trained people to ignore it.</p><p>If your customers do not reliably read email and your WhatsApp open rates are poor, say so, because that is the argument that moves SMS up.</p>","path":"","ord":227},
  {"id":"how-do-our-shopify-shipping-zones-get-into-the-app","cat":"ship","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"How do our Shopify shipping zones get into the app?","a":"<p>Open the app, go to Settings, then Shipping Zones, and hit refresh and save. That syncs your Shopify zones across. You can do it yourself in under a minute.</p><p>We cannot do it for you without Markets access on the collaborator role, which is why you may have been asked either to grant Markets or to press refresh yourself. Either works.</p>","path":"App > Settings > Shipping Zones","ord":228},
  {"id":"can-shipping-be-free-above-a-cart-value-in-a-specific-region-onl","cat":"ship","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Can shipping be free above a cart value, in a specific region only?","a":"<p>Yes. Shipping supports pincode ranges, patterns or exact pincode sets grouped into regions, and then <strong>overrides</strong> on top: a specific product in a specific region can carry a different rate from the standard one, and an override can set a cart value above which shipping becomes free.</p><p>The reason to care: a subscription discount lowers the cart total, which can quietly drop a customer below your free shipping threshold and make the subscription feel more expensive than the one time purchase. Set the threshold against the discounted total, not the list price.</p>","path":"","ord":229},
  {"id":"can-we-turn-subscriptions-off-for-international-orders-but-keep-","cat":"ship","status":"l","asked":3,"q":"Can we turn subscriptions off for international orders but keep bundles on?","a":"<p>Yes. A store whose international shipping rates move can keep subscribe and save domestic only while leaving bundle and save available everywhere, which avoids committing to a fixed subscription price against a rate you cannot predict.</p><p>If you do want international subscriptions, we need Markets access to read the per market shipping profiles before pricing the plans.</p>","path":"","ord":230},
  {"id":"how-should-we-plan-stock-around-subscriptions","cat":"stock","status":"l","asked":4,"q":"How should we plan stock around subscriptions?","a":"<p>Three habits from the stores that had the fewest problems:</p><ul><li><strong>Keep frequencies and durations short.</strong> Monthly delivery on a three month plan means you are planning about a month out. A twelve month plan with quarterly deliveries commits you a year ahead against stock you cannot forecast.</li><li><strong>Use the order lead window.</strong> Delivery orders appear about seven days before the delivery date, so a check on upcoming contracts each week catches shortfalls with time to act.</li><li><strong>Change plans before you run out, not after.</strong> If a product is going short in the next few weeks, pull it from the plan or the bundle now. Existing subscribers keep their plan, and you stop taking new commitments you cannot meet.</li></ul><p>When a delivery does hit a stockout, you have two real options: tell the customer and reschedule that delivery, or cancel and refund it.</p>","path":"","ord":231},
  {"id":"why-did-other-subscription-apps-fail-on-our-otp-or-quick-checkou","cat":"integ","status":"w","asked":4,"q":"Why did other subscription apps fail on our OTP or quick checkout?","a":"<p>Because Shopify does not make its subscription APIs and tooling available in India the way it does in the US and Canada. Most subscription apps on the store are built for those markets, so in India they either break, work around Shopify checkout entirely, or run as a hack on top of it. That is what merchants are describing when they say the last app \"did not work with our checkout\".</p><p>StackBack is built for this market and is a Shopify approved subscriptions partner, which is why it works with standard Shopify checkout and with GoKwik, Shopflo, Razorpay and Shiprocket rather than only one of them.</p>","path":"","ord":232},
  {"id":"what-is-coming-beyond-subscriptions-and-bundles","cat":"integ","status":"w","asked":2,"q":"What is coming beyond subscriptions and bundles?","a":"<p>Memberships, loyalty and trial offers are the stated direction, on the same premise: selling formats that Indian repeat consumption brands need and cannot get cleanly on Shopify today.</p><p>Two things that follow from that focus. The product is built for Indian D2C in repeat consumption categories, not fashion or accessories, so category fit is a real filter. And feature requests from pilot stores in those categories carry weight, which is why \"tell us if this is blocking\" is not a brush off.</p>","path":"","ord":233}
];
